{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:15:47.944109Z","id":"556074fdbdb577235f0c0c99df2ad5daad44b6a019b329e37e3003b4197a5077","body":{"detail":"Each Clax instance runs in exactly one process (its store has one writer, and its realtime state, background tasks and SQLite page cache are in-process), so the fleet scales by spreading instances across pods; one instance's throughput is bounded by one pod. Options: (a) keep this model (the hosted Worker's, one Durable Object per instance); (b) add read replicas of an instance that serve GETs from a snapshot of its pages and forward writes and streams to the owner; (c) port the store to a multi-writer PostgreSQL schema and move realtime delivery to LISTEN/NOTIFY or a broker, which touches every query in crates/clax-store (the alternative recorded on crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs). docs/kubernetes.md, 'How it scales', states (a).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":6},"end":{"line":37},"content_hash":"69dddc540f9cda48fdc9df65822cd71dd2a9d170b02e52811560e517fa459bbc"},"summary":"Horizontal scaling here means more instances across pods, not one instance's load across pods","tags":["status:needs-decision","design","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:15:47.944336Z","id":"6fcfa36bf488619218d5e358e395168e30546c4f01f47395ba0f9c69df176c68","body":{"detail":"Store::open_on gives Exec::Single: no reader pool, no READ_LIMIT interruption, no checkpoint thread, and every store call runs on the calling thread, often a Tokio worker, blocking it for any page read SQLite's cache misses (a PostgreSQL round trip, crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs) and every commit. A slow database stalls runtime threads and serializes an instance's reads behind its writes. A pooled executor over the page VFS would need in-process locking between connections (crates/clax-pagevfs's locks are no-ops).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":489},"end":{"line":513},"content_hash":"bceae97ed7f40a331c80315a747e2369d909b3ac1794c895b9afccd53ef25472"},"summary":"On PostgreSQL an instance's store runs on one connection with jobs inline (Store::open_on)","tags":["perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:15:47.944469Z","id":"73581afdd3b3b90fdd863d950c1f025dd39a802adddfdebf4d1f232c080ee5ca","body":{"detail":"Store::open_with copies a home's database before migrating it (crates/clax-store/src/backup.rs); Store::open_on, which the PostgreSQL backend uses, does not, so an upgrade whose migration goes wrong has no automatic way back. docs/kubernetes.md tells operators to pg_dump first. A copy of the instance's clax_fleet_chunks rows under another file name, taken in one transaction before migrating, would restore the safety net.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":489},"end":{"line":513},"content_hash":"bceae97ed7f40a331c80315a747e2369d909b3ac1794c895b9afccd53ef25472"},"summary":"No copy of an instance's database is made before a migration on PostgreSQL","tags":["data-safety","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:15:47.944847Z","id":"462bbaa8851ae571b3e05b01e3960265e7fe21a46f1d62b1966d5f5f9e3d5251","body":{"detail":"dispatch trusts x-clax-fleet-hop and x-clax-fleet-instance on every request, not only those from other pods. A client can mark its request out of hops (it gets 503 where it would have been forwarded) or name an instance directly with a Host the hosted gate allows for every instance (localhost). Neither starts an instance off its preferred member (Fleet::route), but the headers could be checked against the members' addresses, or signed with a fleet secret.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":754},"end":{"line":795},"content_hash":"c794d05b1a01221c630ba448d297c3b099f047fcc861ecdff3856a4be27f824a"},"summary":"The hop and instance headers are taken from any client","tags":["security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:15:47.944978Z","id":"47521bca0d0a37b3b358dbc30cbed8cd37963bb9f7729ae2bdb02fac6bedd02e","body":{"detail":"Hosted mode counts its abuse limits by client address: CF-Connecting-IP behind Cloudflare ([hosted] behind_cloudflare), else the connection's peer, which for a forwarded request is another pod and behind an Ingress is the controller for every client. Off Cloudflare, every client shares one limit. A trusted-proxy setting that reads X-Forwarded-For from known peers would fix it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":92},"end":{"line":100},"content_hash":"306d9641d8d4a2d830eec513780a51ff9e86edee4304b51cb77f337081d1e745"},"summary":"A forwarded request reaches its owner with the forwarding pod as its peer","tags":["security","ops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800727Z","id":"9c3a66f1824a566593070c0623011e3d976e28e42452ffdab1bdb1cdc3a6329c","body":{"detail":"Failure: a SQLite fleet started without CLAX_KEK (deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml takes clax-secrets as optional and its comment calls the KEK optional) seals ClaxBot model keys under each home's own secrets/kek, made by Kek::load_or_create (crates/clax-store/src/secrets.rs:151). Adding CLAX_KEK later makes Fleet::open write_if_changed the new key over secrets/kek, with no copy of the old one, so every secret sealed before reads as undecryptable. Changing CLAX_KEK on PostgreSQL does the same to every instance. Nothing warns: the file is replaced silently on the next start.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":441},"end":{"line":454},"content_hash":"0026adacf4318a69b2705f3c5a91ac340e44817fef2340cb984b41d3e209bd7a"},"suggested_fix":"Refuse to start an instance whose home holds a different KEK than CLAX_KEK (log both fingerprints and name the recovery), or keep the old key beside it for a re-seal; document in docs/kubernetes.md that CLAX_KEK must be set before an instance first starts and cannot be changed without a rotation.","summary":"Setting CLAX_KEK on a fleet whose SQLite homes already made their own key overwrites each home's KEK and strands every secret sealed under it","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800766Z","id":"d0041c95f3f85e172667d9feda0f35bc776650dc6e3fdb3cf681945a4086cfa3","body":{"detail":"Route::Unavailable carries format!(\"the fleet's database: {e}\") (lines 327, 347, 406), where e is pg::err's \"<SQLSTATE>: <server message>\" or a connection error, plus \"instance {} is held by {holder}\" and \"goes to {pod}\" (333-336, 359-362); unavailable() writes it verbatim into the response body (185-192) for any client, signed in or not. dispatch also echoes the client-sent x-clax-fleet-instance value (767-769). This hands an outside caller the database's errors (role, limits, schema names) and the pods' names, which help with targeting and say nothing a client can act on.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":316},"end":{"line":373},"content_hash":"ada709e755b4e41d1fff2c8cd81d3b292149de53b112e87e15100ff0ec8b2243"},"suggested_fix":"Log the detailed reason with tracing::warn and answer a fixed body (\"this instance is unavailable; retry\") with Retry-After; keep the detail only in the log and, if wanted, the admin port.","summary":"503 bodies send PostgreSQL error text and pod names to anonymous internet clients","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800814Z","id":"fc451012515ec2bacae0917337b16d7e031bee0b11685cd30d32faee86be587a","body":{"detail":"forward opens a plain TcpStream to the owner's advertised address and sends the client's request with its Cookie, Authorization and Clax-Grant headers unchanged. TLS ends at the Ingress, so on a cluster network without encryption (no mesh, no WireGuard CNI) every forwarded request exposes its credentials to anything that can observe pod traffic. docs/kubernetes.md does not mention it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":128},"end":{"line":139},"content_hash":"431c37e1f3048acdf64df2646a5860a75effe730a9500208e80b014885c315a1"},"suggested_fix":"Document in docs/kubernetes.md that pod-to-pod forwarding is plaintext and should run on an encrypted pod network or mesh mTLS; optionally add TLS between pods with a fleet certificate later.","summary":"Forwarded requests carry cookies, bearer tokens and agent credentials between pods over plain TCP","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800844Z","id":"7fed13558708928705b3c596bb215826a027ba89423b5ef01a4f59ba2a3232e2","body":{"detail":"admin_router serves /status without authentication and main.rs binds it to 0.0.0.0:7481 by default (main.rs:80-82). Its JSON names every instance in the catalog and which run on this pod, plus the pod's advertised address: in a multi-tenant fleet that is the customer list, readable from any pod that can reach the port. Probes need only /healthz and /readyz.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":729},"end":{"line":740},"content_hash":"66bfa5138284c969baf958b030327a3552d6aa4a5a77fa34653e056ad560bdf9"},"suggested_fix":"Bind status to loopback by default (kubectl exec or port-forward reach it), or serve only counts on 7481 and the names behind a token; keep /healthz and /readyz on the pod IP for the kubelet.","summary":"/status lists every tenant's instance name to any caller on 0.0.0.0:7481","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:33.800873Z","id":"399a7317056084c4affe500bb377380c598e25a707a6787eb4a17d895ac0271f","body":{"detail":"Catalog::parse refuses only an exact repeat of a site domain or an origin. docs/usage.md (the hosted site_domain paragraph) requires a site domain 'neither the public host nor under it nor above it', and on one daemon that is that daemon's own host; in a fleet, instance A's site_domain = \"example.com\" with instance B at https://b.example.com, or A's at \"sites.example.com\" and B's at \"x.sites.example.com\", passes. Then tenant A's user-authored artifact pages are served on hosts same-site with (or able to set Domain cookies over) tenant B's app origin and sites, which is the cross-site separation site_domain exists to give, broken across tenants.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":176},"end":{"line":189},"content_hash":"500667084ec113faa953e0ad0faf81ec9cb455cd88e072b64e27b15f0c9f5f18"},"suggested_fix":"In Catalog::parse, refuse any site domain equal to, a subdomain of, or a parent of another instance's public host or site domain (comparing labels, not strings), and test each case.","summary":"The catalog lets one tenant's site domain sit under or above another tenant's public host or site domain","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.983835Z","id":"e6ccb94585af54fecf473d3d4069de090657852892063c9910343140c60143e4","body":{"detail":"Failure: deploy/k8s/postgres (hpa.yaml maxReplicas: 10) against a PostgreSQL left at its default max_connections=100 (as deploy/k8s/postgres-dev/postgres.yaml runs postgres:17 with no override) with CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL at its default 16: once 7 pods have each run 16 jobs at once (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:133-160 connects each pool thread lazily and keeps it), the next connection fails with 'sorry, too many clients already', so Pool::run answers 'connecting to PostgreSQL: ...' and the VFS reports SQLITE_IOERR on page reads and commits, the coordinator's heartbeats and renewals fail, and leases lapse. A rolling update's surge pods add to the count. docs/kubernetes.md's table gives the pool size but never the database's connection budget it implies (pods x pool).","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":118},"end":{"line":120},"content_hash":"50b1633c01b937f6d3a943d88929a70905267491812fb106fdc563b252e62d29"},"suggested_fix":"Size the default from the deployment (for example 8, so 10 pods fit under 100 with headroom for the surge and an operator), document 'maxReplicas x CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL + surge < max_connections' in docs/kubernetes.md's PostgreSQL section, and set max_connections in deploy/k8s/postgres-dev; or recommend a PgBouncer in transaction mode (the fleet uses no session state beyond a transaction).","summary":"The default pool of 16 connections per pod times the autoscaler's 10 pods exceeds PostgreSQL's default max_connections of 100","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.984118Z","id":"4a8bd70e5ad33414979db0b47aaf55f53804f11c4b70b34cbee3f950d0249db9","body":{"detail":"PgCoordinator gets the same Pool as PgPages, PgContent and PgAppData. Pool::run (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:173-205) feeds one FIFO queue to CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL threads, so a heartbeat or renewal waits behind every job queued before it: cold-start read-ahead of several instances, 64 MiB commits, 16 MB content reads. Fleet::tick also needs a Tokio worker to run, and instance store calls run inline on Tokio workers and block on Exec::Single's std Mutex (crates/clax-store/src/exec.rs:137-200), so one instance's slow commit can hold several workers. When renewals are held past LEASE_TTL (30 s, crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:33), every instance on the pod stops at once; a heartbeat held past MEMBER_TTL (20 s) drops the pod from the membership.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":227},"end":{"line":230},"content_hash":"0274f66823ec5169c7c36acb5a79768fa2241984760f9b1217540209d377b810"},"suggested_fix":"Give the coordinator its own connection (or a reserved pool thread with its own queue) so control-plane queries never wait on data-plane I/O, and run the ticker on a dedicated thread or runtime; consider a priority lane in Pool for coordinator jobs.","summary":"Heartbeats and lease renewals queue behind page and content I/O on the same pool, so a busy pod can lose every lease it holds","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.984161Z","id":"23f0a975e228cb86b5b0595803ec2f3c230d43424ccee7bed28ee7480b4785dd","body":{"detail":"tick awaits renew(name) for each running instance in turn (lines 577-581); each is a spawn_blocking job on the shared pool and two round trips (prepare, then the UPDATE in coord.rs:221-236). At 1,000 instances and 5 ms per round trip a tick takes 10 s, twice HEARTBEAT_EVERY, and the ticker's interval bursts to catch up; past 30 s of renewal the first leases expire before the last are renewed. A tick that also stops instances (handover, catalog change) awaits their stops (up to STOP_TIMEOUT, 10 s each, in parallel) before the next tick's heartbeat. reload (lines 658-672) also awaits each slot's tokio Mutex, which a start holds across opening and migrating a database.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":563},"end":{"line":639},"content_hash":"d21475fa7aa2cd18badc54b16624abc79dd5b46916fb43910c65198db698a995"},"suggested_fix":"Renew all held leases in one statement (UPDATE clax_fleet_leases SET expires = ... FROM unnest($instances, $epochs) WHERE holder = $pod ... RETURNING instance) and compare the returned set; run stops in a spawned task rather than inside tick; read each slot's config without waiting on a start (keep it next to the router).","summary":"Each tick renews every held lease with its own round trip, one after another, so tick time grows with the instances on a pod","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.984195Z","id":"a8b682958cdb2402b5c3f709ec28cd7462f4aa03cc6d61c8d889e11a970dec70","body":{"detail":"With ROUTE_CACHE at 2 s (line 69), every pod that receives traffic for an instance owned elsewhere queries clax_fleet_leases at least every 2 s for it, and every request arriving while the entry is stale makes its own lookup (spawn_blocking, a pool slot, two round trips), so a burst after expiry sends one query per concurrent request. With no live lease, members() adds another query per request. Database load from routing alone grows as pods x active instances / 2 per second, and these queries wait in the same pool queue as page I/O, adding their wait to forwarded requests' latency. Leases last 30 s and a stale route is already corrected by the owner (a forwarded request it cannot serve is looked up again there).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":316},"end":{"line":350},"content_hash":"4ce089b94a868cf11d13f84c6035f3e3225d7f082d2c33c3c34fe876ff186c2b"},"suggested_fix":"Cache routes for much longer (e.g. until LEASE_TTL, or until a 502/503 from the target), share one in-flight lookup per instance (a tokio OnceCell or watch per entry), and cache members() for a tick; or keep a pod-wide map of all live leases refreshed each tick (one query) and consult PostgreSQL only on a miss.","summary":"A request for an instance held elsewhere looks the lease up in PostgreSQL every 2 s per instance per pod, with no single flight","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.984219Z","id":"4e75db7e1ba8a4bbbc4c5d7abc3c97765776cd8d2d72e0a91aaa627b0a6d4a2d","body":{"detail":"forward connects (tokio TcpStream::connect, which resolves a host name with getaddrinfo on a blocking thread) and handshakes HTTP/1 for every request, then drops the connection. On leases (n-1)/n of requests are forwarded, each paying a TCP handshake on top of the hop; on shards the peer is clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480 (docs/kubernetes.md), so each forward also pays a cluster DNS lookup. Every closed connection leaves a TIME_WAIT socket on the forwarding pod: with Linux's default ephemeral range (32768-60999) and 60 s TIME_WAIT, sustained forwarding beyond about 470 requests/s to one peer fails to connect (EADDRNOTAVAIL), returned as 502.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":128},"end":{"line":145},"content_hash":"3752aa047974b120e730f77ab6a407ae3ab4697efdaa92e3ff79e36c66573857"},"suggested_fix":"Keep a pooled client per peer address (hyper_util::client::legacy::Client with keep-alive, or one HTTP/2 connection per peer multiplexing requests), using a fresh connection only for upgrades; resolve shard peer names once and refresh on failure.","summary":"Each forwarded request opens a new TCP connection, and on shards resolves the peer's name through DNS each time","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.984275Z","id":"d57de89695d4c31d70e425550b0fbcb29edf40ad6c336bb6ef7c0d4cb6d4abb6","body":{"detail":"Catalog::resolve runs three iter().find passes (authority, site domains, default) and Catalog::get one more for a forwarded request, on every request in dispatch (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:754-796). Cheap with tens of instances, it grows with the catalog the fleet is meant to scale (one instance per team or customer).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":219},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"023d55b6c4ef1fd4a097399c496e5dccbb39a6ebbd4fafb4d5fc5070b78ae2f5"},"suggested_fix":"Build HashMaps from authority and from site domain to the instance index once per load (the catalog is reloaded only when it changes), and look a site host up by its parent domain.","summary":"Host resolution scans the catalog linearly up to three times per request","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214570Z","id":"83a7700da432b79f9f9b191f3721bd8f708a8ff8a60419cb7782b9e08bc65c28","body":{"detail":"Failure: a SQLite fleet deployed as docs/kubernetes.md:89-92 shows (clax-secrets with only the setup secret, no CLAX_KEK) lets each instance make its own key in <home>/secrets/kek (clax-store/src/secrets.rs:114-117 Kek::load_or_create, documented as 'never replaced, since every sealed secret depends on it'); an owner then stores ClaxBot model keys. The operator later adds CLAX_KEK to clax-secrets, as deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:62-63 invites ('CLAX_KEK when every shard should seal secrets under one key'), and the pods restart: Fleet::open calls write_if_changed(&home.kek_path(), kek, 0o600) whenever the file differs, replacing the home's key with no copy kept, and every secret sealed before can no longer be opened. The same happens on PostgreSQL to any home whose disk survived when CLAX_KEK is changed, and there the sealed secrets in PostgreSQL are lost to every pod at once: there is no rotation path (old key kept to unseal, new key to seal), and docs/kubernetes.md:127-129 only says to keep the key safe.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":441},"end":{"line":454},"content_hash":"0026adacf4318a69b2705f3c5a91ac340e44817fef2340cb984b41d3e209bd7a"},"suggested_fix":"In Fleet::open, when secrets/kek exists and differs from CLAX_KEK, refuse to start the instance with an error naming both (or keep the home's key and warn), never overwrite it. Document in docs/kubernetes.md that CLAX_KEK must be chosen before the first start on SQLite and cannot be changed, and either add a rotation path (CLAX_KEK_PREVIOUS to unseal and reseal) or state there is none.","summary":"Setting or changing CLAX_KEK overwrites each existing home's own secrets/kek, so every secret sealed under the old key becomes unreadable","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214701Z","id":"96fb105339b7fdee5422bf5abc5a22302cdc25adf3d3bf50af238a7c642c6b6c","body":{"detail":"reload() logs 'the catalog did not reload; keeping the one in effect' on every tick (HEARTBEAT_EVERY, 5 s) for as long as the bad file stays, and neither /readyz nor /status (fleet.rs:715-742) says the catalog in effect is stale. Meanwhile Fleet::new (fleet.rs:199-200) refuses to start on the same file, so a pod that (re)starts - an HPA scale-up, a node drain, a crash, a rollout - exits and crash-loops while its older siblings keep serving the old catalog. docs/kubernetes.md:82-84 says only that a bad catalog 'leaves the previous one in effect'. The documented guard, `clax-fleet check-catalog` (main.rs:52-58), needs a local binary the docs do not say how to get (only the image has one), and on SQLite it needs `--shards` remembered by hand: without it a catalog missing a shard passes the check and is refused by serve.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":645},"end":{"line":655},"content_hash":"35168affff2229e8cf52ca6a6324b01a365a4b78162b3c49a22f267291ff0057"},"suggested_fix":"Add the catalog's state to /status (path, loaded-at, last error) and log a failed reload once per distinct error rather than every tick. Let a starting pod with a bad catalog say so plainly in its exit message and in docs/kubernetes.md. Make check-catalog read CLAX_FLEET_SHARDS like serve, and document running it from the image: `docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/c <image> check-catalog /c/fleet.toml --shards N`.","summary":"A bad catalog is visible only as a warning repeated every 5 s, and any pod that starts while it is mounted crash-loops","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214734Z","id":"d20c5ea9e3b99cc4dfd75804d23775c1ba9235515070c85c8add9c034b4e3a4d","body":{"detail":"Kubernetes updates a mounted ConfigMap on each node at its own time, up to a minute or more later (docs/kubernetes.md:84-85), so for that window some pods lack a newly added instance: a request with its Host that reaches one of them resolves to the default instance (resolve's last line) instead of a 503. The documented way to move a SQLite instance (docs/kubernetes.md:105-111) starts by removing it from the catalog, so for the whole copy its public origin and site hosts are served by the default instance (or 404 without one). Users of that origin see another instance's sign-in and content under their URL.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":226},"end":{"line":241},"content_hash":"0b08a361eec1aead1b9ce6cba6e68c34bf89d1852f7f51d0014c30d6b2025309"},"suggested_fix":"Answer a Host that matches no instance with the default only when it is not a host any instance has ever been known by; simpler: give the catalog an explicit `paused = true` (or `state = \"moving\"`) entry that keeps the instance's origin reserved and answers 503 with Retry-After, use it in the move procedure, and document that the default instance catches unknown hosts during a ConfigMap rollout.","summary":"A host that names no instance in a pod's current catalog is answered by the default instance, so a just-added or just-removed instance's origin serves the default instance's pages","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214771Z","id":"d2e4bf1c2d28a1caca2c6dd74b26ad6bda15aeab22782d34ee90985891c9be79","body":{"detail":"reload() treats a changed shard as 'its shard changed' and stops the instance; the next request goes to the new shard's pod, where start() and open() (fleet.rs:433-440) create a fresh home with ensure_dirs and the catalog's config, with no check that the home was moved there. The result is a blank hosted instance at the same public origin whose /setup accepts the setup secret, which the clax-secrets Secret still holds. Editing `shard` in place is the obvious way to move an instance; docs/kubernetes.md:105-111 asks for remove, copy, re-add, but nothing enforces it.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":661},"end":{"line":668},"content_hash":"9656c307af8d2a5fc633d1df2c8bf2b38e01098d93acacd27106fbe943ce2b67"},"suggested_fix":"On the SQLite backend, have a pod create a home only for an instance it has never served elsewhere: for example, require the catalog to mark a brand-new instance (`create = true`) and otherwise refuse to start an instance whose home is missing on this shard, answering 503 and logging that the home must be copied here first (docs/kubernetes.md:105-111).","summary":"Changing an instance's shard in the catalog stops it and starts a new, empty instance on the new shard, open to /setup again","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214798Z","id":"2f6f4aabb1af8bf9e0ea28b9a7d85192b524cf4b7e20848561fa0df61292f747","body":{"detail":"Hosted::from_config (crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:463-465) reads the instance's setup_secret_env from the process environment, which envFrom (deploy/k8s/*/statefulset.yaml:64-67, deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:73-75) fixes at container start. docs/kubernetes.md:80-82 says an added instance 'starts on its first request', but its CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_<NAME> added to clax-secrets reaches no running pod, so /setup stays closed. `clax serve` warns in that case ('hosted mode: no setup secret in the environment, so /setup stays closed', crates/clax-cli/src/serve.rs:64-69); Fleet::open does not. The same holds for a GitHub client secret and for rotating any of them: rotation needs `kubectl rollout restart`, which on SQLite takes each shard's instances down for its restart.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":455},"end":{"line":465},"content_hash":"de9b6983e8885115c728331345b4446269fc731fcdd12881de435dc638cb9627"},"suggested_fix":"Log the same warning in Fleet::open when the setup secret is absent, and document in docs/kubernetes.md that adding an instance's secret or rotating one needs a rollout restart. Better: mount clax-secrets as a volume and read secrets from files at instance start, so an added instance needs no restart.","summary":"An instance added to a running fleet gets no setup secret until every pod restarts, and the fleet logs nothing about it","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214825Z","id":"043801257ad47f33fb9d61f42581344ace1b7e78d12c55f44aff8b9f69d467ee","body":{"detail":"tick() sets healthy = false on the first heartbeat error, and ready() (fleet.rs:234-236) answers /readyz 503 from then until a heartbeat succeeds, while lease renewals tolerate RENEW_FAILURES (4) errors in a row before an instance stops (fleet.rs:64-65). A PostgreSQL failover or a short network fault hits every pod's heartbeat in the same 5 s tick, so with the readinessProbe at periodSeconds: 5 (deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:87-91) the clax Service can lose all endpoints and the Ingress answers 503 for every instance, including those whose leases are still valid and that would answer reads.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":566},"end":{"line":576},"content_hash":"c20b3bf6c07a6ea2d0e0501e778e9a7387324155274b5923cf19bd27b36a9f9d"},"suggested_fix":"Mark the pod unhealthy only after the same RENEW_FAILURES consecutive heartbeat failures (or once its membership would lapse, MEMBER_TTL), and set the readinessProbe's failureThreshold to match.","summary":"One failed heartbeat makes a pod not ready, so a database blip of a few seconds takes every pod out of the Service at once","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214896Z","id":"74d56d45b73b58523464374a4d8ea5d04e46060b0c2ae90c014fbe24a979dd5d","body":{"detail":"main() installs tracing_subscriber::fmt() with no JSON option (the workspace's tracing-subscriber has only the env-filter feature, Cargo.toml:89), so a cluster's log pipeline (Loki, Cloud Logging, Elasticsearch) cannot index fields such as instance or epoch. Only the fleet's own events carry `instance` (fleet.rs:416, 420, 540, 556, 592, 635); the request path (dispatch, Fleet::handle) and the instance's Core and its tasks run with no span, so an error from inside one of twenty instances on a pod cannot be told apart. RUST_LOG, which sets the filter, is not in docs/kubernetes.md's variable table (181-204).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":127},"end":{"line":132},"content_hash":"74ebcb2cad1e034c8cacfd6a6da9c80647e8be96062cae63839d1f9eea535551"},"suggested_fix":"Add CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json (tracing-subscriber's json feature), wrap each instance's request handling and its Core tasks in an `instance` span, and add RUST_LOG to the docs' table.","summary":"Logs are plain text only, and an instance's own log lines do not name the instance","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214924Z","id":"3e57c16e8984015b4fd72e778b6a339e1820c8c5286cb9c86b51dfc45f1352f4","body":{"detail":"The admin port serves only /healthz, /readyz and /status (JSON of names). There is no Prometheus endpoint: no counters for requests by instance and route (local, forwarded, 503), forward errors (the 502s that Fleet::handle reacts to), instance starts and their duration, failed starts, lease renew failures and losses, handovers, catalog reload failures, or PostgreSQL pool waits. Each of these is today only a log line, and the HPA (deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml) can only use CPU.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":712},"end":{"line":742},"content_hash":"3d867c564ce4fbb82d7e80454cf2cbd105a1623f444b6d76dfe8f5b460576041"},"suggested_fix":"Serve /metrics in Prometheus text format on the admin port with those counters and gauges (instances running, leases held, pool in use), and document a ServiceMonitor or pod annotations for scraping.","summary":"The fleet exposes no metrics, so an operator can neither alert on lease losses, forwards and start failures nor autoscale on anything but CPU","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:36:07.214951Z","id":"fa961b0191b6f46e4e325e36fc77b7511863f8f0c62eca13d2118131cfd3ca5e","body":{"detail":"FleetConfig.version is documented as 'The version /healthz reports' (fleet.rs:124-125), but /healthz answers a bare 'ok' (line 718) and /status (730-739) gives only pod, advertise, ready, instances and running. During a rolling upgrade, or with pods that pulled different :latest builds, an operator cannot tell which version each pod runs, and cannot see a SQLite pod's ordinal or shard count either.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":715},"end":{"line":742},"content_hash":"0046b7bd83d218318afdb40d3989144054a57458801d184cc51f5a82ba500d14"},"suggested_fix":"Add version (CARGO_PKG_VERSION and the git revision), backend, ordinal/shards and the catalog's load state to /status, and either report the version in /healthz or correct the field's doc comment.","summary":"No endpoint reports the running version, although FleetConfig.version says /healthz does, and /status omits the backend, shard and catalog state","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:03.388720Z","id":"0fc08648dadfc716bcbdc1c301f524816b83a623f27a05167023927828e5144d","body":{"detail":"A container restart (OOM kill, failed liveness probe) keeps the pod's name and IP. Leases its previous process held still name this pod until LEASE_TTL (30s, crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:33). In Fleet::route a lease naming this pod that is not running and not stopping falls through (the `Ok(_) => {}` arm, fleet.rs:344) to rendezvous hashing. For an instance the hashing prefers here, start() re-acquires at a new epoch and all is well. But for one that was here although another member is now preferred (that member joined less than coord::SETTLE ago, or the handover, which runs every third tick at fleet.rs:583-584, had not yet run), the request goes: any pod -> lease holder (this pod, hop 1) -> preferred member B (hop 2) -> B sees the lease naming this pod and is out of hops -> 503 (fleet.rs:333-336). Every request for that instance is refused until the dead incarnation's lease expires, although this process is the only one that could take it and knows it holds nothing it does not run. Not covered by crates/clax-fleet/tests/leases.rs, whose processes never restart.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":326},"end":{"line":345},"content_hash":"3f8d96af2f4dfdd415cab4518af733a0b7c2da989bcf495ea64f9e4dc258beee"},"suggested_fix":"On start-up (before the first tick in crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs run), release every lease that names this pod: `UPDATE clax_fleet_leases SET expires = now() WHERE holder = $1` (and the MemCoordinator equivalent, as a new Coordinator method), so the previous incarnation's holds are free at once and each instance goes where hashing prefers. Add a leases.rs test that drops a Process and starts another with the same pod name.","summary":"A process restarted under the same pod name defers its previous incarnation's live leases to hashing, leaving those instances 503 until the lease expires","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:03.388791Z","id":"6522bc922e83f96d47389641803064670b1afa891e71c2492ab2550aafa8ba80","body":{"detail":"Fleet::route reads `draining` once (fleet.rs:298) and, on leases, then awaits coord.lease and coord.members (fleet.rs:326, 346) before start() creates the slot (fleet.rs:381) and acquires the lease. Fleet::drain snapshots the slot names (fleet.rs:693) after main.rs sleeps --drain-delay (5s by default, crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:121-123). A request that read draining=false and whose two database calls take longer than the delay (the database under load, which is when pods are being cycled) creates its slot after the snapshot, so drain does not stop it; start() itself never rechecks draining. The process then exits (main.rs:307-312) with that instance's core unstopped (its audit journal segment not closed) and its lease held, so the instance is refused for up to LEASE_TTL. The comment at fleet.rs:691-692 says a start in progress ends first; that holds only for a slot that existed when names were listed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":684},"end":{"line":702},"content_hash":"309ed8c6df9de51b1212c27b57a519c077d5275b86f3c545f14e82fbe5ceeebf"},"suggested_fix":"In Fleet::start, after taking the slot's running lock, return Route::Unavailable(\"this process is stopping\") when self.draining is set, before acquiring the lease. Then every start either sees draining or holds the lock before drain's stop of that slot, and drain's snapshot can no longer miss one.","summary":"A start whose slot is made after drain lists the slots is never stopped: the process exits holding the lease with the core unstopped","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:03.388831Z","id":"2b58ea4e68c69e4e0e2876bb0b20c29b6a2dc4020ef22861f3aa473a969a21b9","body":{"detail":"Fleet::stop calls Core::stop_bounded(STOP_TIMEOUT) and only logs when it answers false. Core::stop_bounded's contract (crates/clax-server/src/core.rs:268-271) is that on false the store's queued calls and threads are left running, 'which only a process about to exit may leave behind'. The fleet also stops instances in a running process: a config change, a shard change or a catalog removal (Fleet::reload, fleet.rs:645-674) and, on leases, a handover. On Storage::Disk (the SQLite backend) nothing fences the leftover store: the next request starts the instance again (Fleet::start) with Core::start on the same home while the old Store's writer and threads still run, so two Stores of one instance write the same database, and the new core's in-memory state (live pages, grant index, loaded at AppState::assemble) misses whatever the old one commits after it loaded. On PostgreSQL the fence (fence.lose(), fleet.rs:546) and the dropped VFS registration stop the leftover's writes, so only the SQLite backend is exposed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":537},"end":{"line":541},"content_hash":"30a1e215baa8f5633ae1817c9683d469d9e869054657df0d2cb8f67cf7ccd720"},"suggested_fix":"When stop_bounded answers false on Storage::Disk, keep the slot refusing starts (leave `stopping` set, or mark the slot poisoned) until the old store's shutdown task has ended, by keeping its JoinHandle (stop_store's spawn_blocking) and waiting for it before the next start; or have Core::stop_bounded hand back the unfinished shutdown so the fleet can await it.","summary":"After a stop whose store did not finish in time, the next start opens a second store on the same SQLite home","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:03.388943Z","id":"440a39279e3d28ace5ceedf2f179d3cc04e4a840b0157db55daa5f8684df3f5b","body":{"detail":"Fleet::open opens the instance's database with clax_pagevfs::open (Limits::default, MAX_TRANSACTION_BYTES = 64 MiB, crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs:115-131); a transaction that dirties more fails with SQLITE_FULL and rolls back. clax-store's migrations rebuild tables by copying them (CREATE ... _NN; INSERT INTO ... SELECT; ALTER TABLE ... RENAME, e.g. inbox_items at crates/clax-store/src/migrations.rs:939-943 and 2456-2460), and Store::open_on runs migrations at start. A future migration of that shape on an instance whose table exceeds 64 MiB (an inbox or audit table of a long-lived hosted instance) fails every time Fleet::start runs it, so after an upgrade that instance answers 503 ('did not start', fleet.rs:419-426) on every request, with nothing an operator can set to get past it. Limits::default's doc names VACUUM as the known case; migrations are the one at start.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":497},"end":{"line":508},"content_hash":"13e9ba4b35dccff9e434aeefa1abc9cab4e701d0b44623e66417cb5c81ca77fa"},"suggested_fix":"Give FleetConfig (and a clax-fleet flag) the page VFS Limits and open the database with clax_pagevfs::open_with, with a larger max_transaction_bytes for the instance's database than for app data stores; and/or note in docs/kubernetes.md's upgrade steps that migrations are bounded by it. A test in crates/clax-pagevfs/src/tests.rs that a rebuild of a table larger than the cap fails with SQLITE_FULL documents the limit.","summary":"On PostgreSQL an instance's database opens with clax-pagevfs's default 64 MiB per-transaction cap, so a migration that rebuilds a larger table can never complete and the instance cannot start","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.293256Z","id":"6e24cef0bd15f86d4e14a1121ce6210f90866ccaee683a5a47e3e64b4e3f80d6","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:441-453 calls write_if_changed(&home.kek_path(), kek, 0o600) whenever cfg.kek is set; write_if_changed (fleet.rs:800-818) renames a temp file over the path when the bytes differ, keeping no copy and logging nothing. Without CLAX_KEK, crates/clax-store/src/secrets.rs:133-175 (load_or_make) creates a per-home key. deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:62-67 takes clax-secrets as optional and describes CLAX_KEK as set 'when every shard should seal secrets under one key'.","kind":"comment","references":"9c3a66f1824a566593070c0623011e3d976e28e42452ffdab1bdb1cdc3a6329c","summary":"confirmed: a set or changed CLAX_KEK silently overwrites each home's existing secrets/kek","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.293559Z","id":"7f16b86b062307f3c337734541cd3d5713adbabd1feeed9b861128bc5961256f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:184-191 unavailable() writes why verbatim into a text/plain 503 body. Route::Unavailable is built from format!(\"the fleet's database: {e}\") at three sites in Fleet::route (lines ~327, ~347, ~406) and from messages naming l.holder and p.pod (~333-336, ~359-362); pg.rs:233-237 err() formats '<SQLSTATE>: <server message>'. dispatch (fleet.rs ~766-769) echoes the client-sent instance header with {name:?}. No authentication is consulted before these answers.","kind":"comment","references":"d0041c95f3f85e172667d9feda0f35bc776650dc6e3fdb3cf681945a4086cfa3","summary":"confirmed: 503 bodies carry database error text, holder and pod names to any client","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.294206Z","id":"32cb4adb474f2722ec7fe448810ce6df9ec61a58beb9d2f3e1aacb5bcba178c6","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs:128-145 connects tokio::net::TcpStream to addr and hyper http1-handshakes on it with no TLS; strip_hop_by_hop (proxy.rs:55-75) removes only Connection-named and hop-by-hop headers, so Cookie, Authorization and Clax-Grant pass through. docs/kubernetes.md's Limits section (~217-220) mentions forwarding but not that it is unencrypted.","kind":"comment","references":"fc451012515ec2bacae0917337b16d7e031bee0b11685cd30d32faee86be587a","summary":"confirmed: forward sends the client's request over a plain TcpStream with its credentials","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.294493Z","id":"771a00eced6ca5ec5906ea16ec56dc19c7629757ddb328a0322e313ed4d93cf2","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:729-739 returns pod, advertise, and catalog().instances names plus running, with no auth layer on admin_router; crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:81 defaults --admin-listen to 0.0.0.0:7481; docs/kubernetes.md:184-187 documents the same.","kind":"comment","references":"7fed13558708928705b3c596bb215826a027ba89423b5ef01a4f59ba2a3232e2","summary":"confirmed: /status lists every catalog instance name unauthenticated on 0.0.0.0:7481","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:15.294768Z","id":"cfc789f7e898959338c70651ab7c8ae69e495a617ab81ffe85dc0155f0730880","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs:169-186 inserts each authority into authorities and each site domain into domains and refuses only a duplicate within the same set, so a site domain equal to, under, or above another instance's public host or site domain passes. The under/above rule exists only per instance, against that instance's own public host (crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:777). docs/usage.md:200 states the rule.","kind":"comment","references":"399a7317056084c4affe500bb377380c598e25a707a6787eb4a17d895ac0271f","summary":"confirmed: Catalog::parse checks only exact repeats, in separate sets for origins and site domains","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.484630Z","id":"21556b756a9a27dc5cc5393a291ac8dc77f137cd9e8d9f75596a7a7cf43914df","body":{"detail":"Holds: crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:118-120 defaults CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL to 16; crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:133-160 spawns size threads that each keep the client they connect on their first job (pg.rs:179-187); deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml has minReplicas: 2, maxReplicas: 10; deploy/k8s/postgres-dev/postgres.yaml:34 runs image postgres:17 with no max_connections override; docs/kubernetes.md:202 gives only 'connections per pod (16)'. Severity: at minReplicas the fleet opens at most 32 connections; reaching 100 takes the HPA scaling to 7 pods on CPU and every pod having had 16 database jobs in flight at once, against the dev database (managed PostgreSQL sizes max_connections by instance class). That is a sizing/documentation risk on a scaled-out deployment, so a concern rather than a blocker.","kind":"comment","references":"e6ccb94585af54fecf473d3d4069de090657852892063c9910343140c60143e4","summary":"downgraded: concern — the arithmetic holds (10 x 16 > 100), but it needs 7+ pods each saturating 16 concurrent jobs against an unconfigured database: a capacity-planning risk, not a defect hit before merge","tags":["verified:downgraded","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.486728Z","id":"ddeed532ce8af9b52bb107cb8c708cf65b1bf9bd74d495d0862bd7ea0949d01f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:227-230 builds one Pool and hands pool.clone() to PgCoordinator and the same pool to Storage::Postgres (used for PgPages and PgContent at crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:493-494). crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:129-145 and 195-197 feed one mpsc channel to all threads, so jobs run in arrival order with no priority. LEASE_TTL is 30 s and MEMBER_TTL 20 s (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:29-33). Fleet::tick runs on the Tokio runtime (main.rs:271-275).","kind":"comment","references":"4a8bd70e5ad33414979db0b47aaf55f53804f11c4b70b34cbee3f950d0249db9","summary":"confirmed: the coordinator shares the data pool's single FIFO queue, so renewals and heartbeats wait behind page and content jobs","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.486977Z","id":"64b1e5103fd0d3aa9f15d6445eac99207fcfb2b9734b68d7120eee2f45d5d9b4","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:577-581 awaits self.renew for each running instance sequentially; renew (610-639) calls coord.renew, a Pool::call (spawn_blocking) running a &str execute, prepared then run (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:221-236). tick awaits join_all(stops) (601-605) before returning, and the ticker (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:271-275) uses tokio::time::interval with the default MissedTickBehavior::Burst. reload (658-660) awaits each slot.running lock, as renew does at 618.","kind":"comment","references":"23f0a975e228cb86b5b0595803ec2f3c230d43424ccee7bed28ee7480b4785dd","summary":"confirmed: tick awaits each renewal in turn, each a pool job of two round trips, and awaits its stops before returning","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.487224Z","id":"deffb26d2c000b3dc2f8772064c72bed91eeebc8e6673e22a846ac11474e4627","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:69 ROUTE_CACHE = 2 s; cached_route (257-261) expires the entry and nothing coalesces concurrent misses: each request reaching 326 calls coord.lease, a pool job, and 346 calls coord.members when no other pod holds it. Requests with hops > 0 skip the cache (321).","kind":"comment","references":"a8b682958cdb2402b5c3f709ec28cd7462f4aa03cc6d61c8d889e11a970dec70","summary":"confirmed: routes are cached 2 s, and each request on a stale or missing entry makes its own lease lookup, plus members() with no live lease","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.487474Z","id":"dabf01de2b553b0c79f66c5627d6ad2f8f9196fc85366847604be555398883c2","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs:128-145 calls tokio::net::TcpStream::connect(addr) and hyper::client::conn::http1::handshake for every forwarded request, with no pool; the sender is dropped when forward returns, so the forwarding side closes first and holds the TIME_WAIT. On shards the peer is clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480 (docs/kubernetes.md:97), a name resolved on each connect. Linux's default net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range is 32768-60999 and TIME_WAIT lasts 60 s (https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/networking/ip-sysctl.txt).","kind":"comment","references":"4e75db7e1ba8a4bbbc4c5d7abc3c97765776cd8d2d72e0a91aaa627b0a6d4a2d","summary":"confirmed: forward opens a new TcpStream and HTTP/1 handshake per request and drops it after","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.487972Z","id":"27ad4436fd4e41daaaf6d730b59a3dddb71386db5899299eaa64d69f946f4d0c","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs:219-221 get is iter().find by name; resolve (226-241) runs iter().find over authority (229), site domains (232-237) and default (240). Dispatch calls them per request (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs around 754-796). reload (fleet.rs:658-661) also calls catalog.get for every running instance each tick.","kind":"comment","references":"d57de89695d4c31d70e425550b0fbcb29edf40ad6c336bb6ef7c0d4cb6d4abb6","summary":"confirmed: resolve makes up to three linear passes and get one more","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.885618Z","id":"b13703696bf481c11fa7384fc92accae2800e543158fe64c20c185ca373e4ef0","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:441-454: when self.cfg.kek is Some, Fleet::open calls write_if_changed(&home.kek_path(), kek, 0o600); write_if_changed (fleet.rs:800-815) returns early only when the file already equals the bytes, otherwise writes a temp file and renames it over the old one, keeping no copy. crates/clax-store/src/secrets.rs:114-116 documents the home key as 'never replaced, since every sealed secret depends on it'. deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:62-67 takes CLAX_KEK from the optional clax-secrets via envFrom, and docs/kubernetes.md:89-92 creates that Secret with no CLAX_KEK, so a later addition replaces each home's self-made key. No rotation path exists (grep for a previous-key variable in crates/clax-fleet/src finds none). The Failure: line is concrete.","kind":"comment","references":"83a7700da432b79f9f9b191f3721bd8f708a8ff8a60419cb7782b9e08bc65c28","summary":"confirmed: setting or changing CLAX_KEK overwrites an existing home's secrets/kek","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.886592Z","id":"d03a095f4deb51974541d85ffe11301bc93ec0c25fc4dd6fd51b34e6e2dcc162","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:645-655: reload() logs 'the catalog did not reload; keeping the one in effect' on each Err, and tick() (fleet.rs:563-600) runs it every tick; main.rs:271 ticks every coord::HEARTBEAT_EVERY (coord.rs:31, 5 s). /status (fleet.rs:730-739) returns only pod, advertise, ready, instances and running. Fleet::new (fleet.rs:199-200) returns the Catalog::load error, which main() (main.rs:138-143) turns into exit failure. check-catalog's --shards is a plain flag (main.rs:53-58) while serve reads CLAX_FLEET_SHARDS (main.rs:93-95), and Catalog::parse accepts an instance with no shard when shards is None (catalog.rs:133-141; test at catalog.rs:329-330).","kind":"comment","references":"96fb105339b7fdee5422bf5abc5a22302cdc25adf3d3bf50af238a7c642c6b6c","summary":"confirmed: a bad catalog warns every tick, is absent from /status, and crash-loops any pod that starts","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.886867Z","id":"a1d772afd1463b667fb08fee1fed3b071adf81070a79a232eff585d3c7736370","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs:226-241: resolve() returns the instance whose authority or site domain matches, else self.instances.iter().find(|i| i.default). docs/kubernetes.md:66 ('answers hosts no instance names') and 105-111 (move = remove from catalog, copy, re-add) mean the moved instance's origin resolves to the default during the copy; docs/kubernetes.md:84-85 states the ConfigMap update lag. Mounted ConfigMap updates are eventually consistent per kubelet sync (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/#mounted-configmaps-are-updated-automatically).","kind":"comment","references":"d20c5ea9e3b99cc4dfd75804d23775c1ba9235515070c85c8add9c034b4e3a4d","summary":"confirmed: an unmatched Host falls to the default instance, including a removed or not-yet-loaded instance's origin","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.887145Z","id":"4d8377ed97b5165d1516aa13a8447266d4ba9c190c0ee75bba2cf723e89444cb","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:661-668: reload() stops a running instance with 'its shard changed' when i.shard != Some(ordinal). Fleet::open (fleet.rs:433-440) runs home.ensure_dirs() and writes config.toml with no check that the home holds data. docs/kubernetes.md:79-82 itself says that when an instance's shard changes 'it stops, and its next request starts it again with the new entry', while the move procedure (docs/kubernetes.md:105-111) needs remove, copy, re-add; nothing enforces it.","kind":"comment","references":"d2e4bf1c2d28a1caca2c6dd74b26ad6bda15aeab22782d34ee90985891c9be79","summary":"confirmed: a shard edit stops the instance and the new shard opens a blank home","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.887417Z","id":"28acb1eeda1f3ea89dd88870a83595ce43a872e7165e7404d1e93a8a4e98548e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-http/src/hosted.rs:463-465: Hosted::from_config reads std::env::var(&c.setup_secret_env), and fleet.rs:459 calls it with no setup_open() check, whereas crates/clax-cli/src/serve.rs:64-69 warns 'hosted mode: no setup secret in the environment, so /setup stays closed'. deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:64-67 and deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:73-75 load clax-secrets with envFrom; an environment variable from a Secret is not updated in a running container (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/#using-secrets-as-environment-variables).","kind":"comment","references":"2f6f4aabb1af8bf9e0ea28b9a7d85192b524cf4b7e20848561fa0df61292f747","summary":"confirmed: an added instance's setup secret reaches no running pod, and the fleet does not warn","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.887701Z","id":"3cce6b124526484cb63f4d419a3fb13af681301cb64e8344c0d2281092403724","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:569-575 does set healthy=false on the first heartbeat error and back to true on the next success, 5 s later (coord.rs:31). But the pod leaves the Service only when the kubelet marks it NotReady, after failureThreshold consecutive probe failures, whose default is 3 (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/#configure-probes); deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:87-91 sets only periodSeconds: 5. A blip of a few seconds fails one heartbeat, so /readyz answers 503 for about one probe and the pod stays ready; it takes about three consecutive failed heartbeats (around 15 s), close to the 4 renewals (20 s, fleet.rs:65) after which instances stop anyway. The headline and the suggested 'set failureThreshold to match' miss the existing default.","kind":"comment","references":"043801257ad47f33fb9d61f42581344ace1b7e78d12c55f44aff8b9f69d467ee","summary":"refuted: one failed heartbeat does not take a pod out of the Service; the probe's default failureThreshold of 3 does","tags":["verified:refuted","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:04.888658Z","id":"0ed361c4311dcd5c81a2d14991e629eab8397f7ff5ea9f82463dd90845727188","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:127-132 installs tracing_subscriber::fmt() with an EnvFilter only; Cargo.toml:89 gives tracing-subscriber only the env-filter feature (no json). grep -n 'info_span|instrument|span!' crates/clax-fleet/src/*.rs finds nothing; instance fields appear only on fleet.rs:416, 420, 540, 550, 556, 592, 635. grep -n RUST_LOG docs/kubernetes.md finds nothing.","kind":"comment","references":"74d56d45b73b58523464374a4d8ea5d04e46060b0c2ae90c014fbe24a979dd5d","summary":"confirmed: plain-text logs only, no instance span on request or core paths, RUST_LOG undocumented","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:21.158588Z","id":"ccab775b85fbbdf1acae49dcc6316a6480a6d05ddf6aa5738ea4e30cee05c638","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:712-742: admin_router routes only /healthz, /readyz and /status. grep -rn 'metrics|prometheus' crates/clax-fleet/src/*.rs crates/clax-fleet/Cargo.toml finds nothing. deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml scales on the cpu Resource metric only.","kind":"comment","references":"3e57c16e8984015b4fd72e778b6a339e1820c8c5286cb9c86b51dfc45f1352f4","summary":"confirmed: the admin port has no metrics endpoint","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:21.159360Z","id":"165c7e62fa478ecfd2d80281a85714b4b573cd056a76c546944fa3845cbfc784","body":{"detail":"FleetConfig.version (fleet.rs:124-125) is handed to each instance's CoreConfig (fleet.rs:467 'version: self.cfg.version'), and the instance's /healthz (crates/clax-server/src/router.rs:659, crates/clax-server/src/routes/health.rs:11-40) returns version, pid, started_at, commit and build; crates/clax-server/tests/api_auth.rs:53 healthz_reports_version_and_allows_any_origin tests it. docs/kubernetes.md:186 says 'An instance's own /healthz answers on 7480 at its origin.' So the doc comment is accurate and the version is reported. What remains true is narrower: the admin port's /healthz (fleet.rs:718) answers a bare 'ok', and /status (fleet.rs:730-739) omits version, backend, ordinal/shards and catalog state — a suggestion, not this finding's claim.","kind":"comment","references":"fa961b0191b6f46e4e325e36fc77b7511863f8f0c62eca13d2118131cfd3ca5e","summary":"refuted: an instance's /healthz reports the version, as FleetConfig.version's doc says","tags":["verified:refuted","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:24.242335Z","id":"f62a9b5f116c09ecaf29e5e7cb21941b85f556ec572e740b4a9d05a25fdb4979","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:326-344: a live lease naming self.cfg.pod that is not stopping matches `Ok(_) => {}` and falls through to coord::preferred (fleet.rs:352-362). MAX_HOPS = 2 (fleet.rs:62). Traced: request at A (hop 0) -> A forwards to preferred B (hop 1) -> B sees lease holder A, forwards back (hop 2) -> A cannot forward, answers 'goes to B, not here' (fleet.rs:360); from a third pod X: X -> A (hop 1) -> B (hop 2) -> B answers 'held by A' (fleet.rs:334). Nothing frees it sooner: renew (fleet.rs:609-640) runs only for self.running(), so the old lease lapses at LEASE_TTL = 30s (coord.rs:33); main.rs run (main.rs:263-264) does only a tick before serving, with no release of leases naming the pod. The coordinators already treat a same-named restart as a re-acquire at a new epoch (coord.rs:198-200 and MemCoordinator::acquire coord.rs:331-335), but route never reaches start() for an instance hashing places elsewhere. The handover that would set B preferred while A holds is gated on p.age >= SETTLE (fleet.rs:590), so the window exists. No leases.rs test restarts a pod under the same name (test list: crates/clax-fleet/tests/leases.rs:47,85,109,136,154).","kind":"comment","references":"0fc08648dadfc716bcbdc1c301f524816b83a623f27a05167023927828e5144d","summary":"confirmed: a restarted process defers its old incarnation's live lease to hashing, and the hop limit turns that into 503 until LEASE_TTL","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:24.242744Z","id":"41daf5e52c8393419aebd8584bc309a979edd5bcbb10ccfaa6f752353cbb49ad","body":{"detail":"Fleet::route reads draining once (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:298) and uses that local copy at fleet.rs:348 and 364 after awaiting coord.lease (326) and coord.members (346). Fleet::start (fleet.rs:379-428) creates the slot at fleet.rs:381 and acquires the lease with no draining check. Fleet::drain snapshots `lock(&self.slots).keys()` at fleet.rs:693 after main.rs sleeps drain_delay (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:303-304, default 5s at main.rs:121-123), then main.rs:308-316 aborts the ticker and exits. A slot inserted after the snapshot is not in `names`, so it is neither stopped nor its lease released. The same gap also hits a slot that did exist: if drain's stop takes the running lock first it finds None (fleet.rs:533-536) and returns, and the waiting start then acquires the lease and opens the core. The comment at fleet.rs:691-692 holds only when the start holds the lock first.","kind":"comment","references":"6522bc922e83f96d47389641803064670b1afa891e71c2492ab2550aafa8ba80","summary":"confirmed: start() never rechecks draining, so a start whose slot is made after drain's snapshot is never stopped","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:24.243072Z","id":"30358d611b736e1b6cf4267f1cf9636b5dc3b52620017778fd8b0ee5df3f4fda","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:537-541 only logs when Core::stop_bounded(STOP_TIMEOUT) answers false; crates/clax-server/src/core.rs:268-271 documents false as the store's queued calls and threads left running, 'which only a process about to exit may leave behind'. Fleet::stop is called in a running process from tick (fleet.rs:597-604) for reload's config/shard/catalog changes (fleet.rs:645-674), lost leases and the handover. The Disk arm of Fleet::open (fleet.rs:482-487) calls Core::start on the same home with no lock: `grep -rn 'flock|try_lock_exclusive|lock_exclusive|fd_lock|LOCK_EX' crates/clax-server/src crates/clax-store/src crates/clax-core/src` finds no file lock in code (only .qual text). On PostgreSQL fence.lose() (fleet.rs:546) runs after the bounded stop and the Running's `_db` registration is dropped (fleet.rs:553), which the Disk arm (`_db: None`, fleet.rs:486) does not have. Disk is a production combination (shards placement); leases always pair with Postgres (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:231).","kind":"comment","references":"2b58ea4e68c69e4e0e2876bb0b20c29b6a2dc4020ef22861f3aa473a969a21b9","summary":"confirmed: on Storage::Disk nothing fences a store stop_bounded left running from the next start on the same home","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:41:24.244468Z","id":"cd73dcd318fc7ce52e0165180c1ce5470b317f84a317cf55b618249610767471","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:499 calls clax_pagevfs::open(\"db\", db), which uses Limits::default (crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs:122-131: max_transaction_bytes = MAX_TRANSACTION_BYTES = 64 MiB, lib.rs:117); past it the write fails with SQLITE_FULL (lib.rs:19-20, 263) and the doc names VACUUM. Store::open_on runs migrations at fleet.rs:500-505, each in its transaction (crates/clax-store/src/migrations.rs:2023 `tx.execute_batch(...)`), and existing migrations rebuild tables by copying (inbox_items_30 at migrations.rs:939-943, inbox_items_37 at 2456-2460). An open error makes start answer 'did not start' (fleet.rs:419-426) on every request. No FleetConfig field or flag sets the VFS Limits (`grep -n open_with crates/clax-fleet/src` finds nothing). The failure needs a future migration of that shape on a table over the cap, so concern (not blocker) is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"440a39279e3d28ace5ceedf2f179d3cc04e4a840b0157db55daa5f8684df3f5b","summary":"confirmed: the instance database opens with default Limits (64 MiB per transaction) and migrations run as one transaction at start","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:42:05.226782Z","id":"5a016d4debdb9680318aa931d7263f6b1336d8b192571a7559ffa285f80582a3","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Refuted by verification reply 3cce6b124526484cb63f4d419a3fb13af681301cb64e8344c0d2281092403724","supersedes":"043801257ad47f33fb9d61f42581344ace1b7e78d12c55f44aff8b9f69d467ee","tags":["reason:invalid","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:42:05.307Z","id":"e32848b0e6e76bff31929de917fbc32201347b9fd683d7411a69ab185e81d346","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Refuted by verification reply 165c7e62fa478ecfd2d80281a85714b4b573cd056a76c546944fa3845cbfc784","supersedes":"fa961b0191b6f46e4e325e36fc77b7511863f8f0c62eca13d2118131cfd3ca5e","tags":["reason:invalid","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:42:05.386854Z","id":"16a32d00d46e3cee91159c8cd1634003a6feae5be12cfaeb18cbd90f1b950b42","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/postgres (hpa.yaml maxReplicas: 10) against a PostgreSQL left at its default max_connections=100 (as deploy/k8s/postgres-dev/postgres.yaml runs postgres:17 with no override) with CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL at its default 16: once 7 pods have each run 16 jobs at once (crates/clax-fleet/src/pg.rs:133-160 connects each pool thread lazily and keeps it), the next connection fails with 'sorry, too many clients already', so Pool::run answers 'connecting to PostgreSQL: ...' and the VFS reports SQLITE_IOERR on page reads and commits, the coordinator's heartbeats and renewals fail, and leases lapse. A rolling update's surge pods add to the count. docs/kubernetes.md's table gives the pool size but never the database's connection budget it implies (pods x pool).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":118},"end":{"line":120},"content_hash":"50b1633c01b937f6d3a943d88929a70905267491812fb106fdc563b252e62d29"},"suggested_fix":"Size the default from the deployment (for example 8, so 10 pods fit under 100 with headroom for the surge and an operator), document 'maxReplicas x CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL + surge < max_connections' in docs/kubernetes.md's PostgreSQL section, and set max_connections in deploy/k8s/postgres-dev; or recommend a PgBouncer in transaction mode (the fleet uses no session state beyond a transaction).","summary":"The default pool of 16 connections per pod times the autoscaler's 10 pods exceeds PostgreSQL's default max_connections of 100","supersedes":"e6ccb94585af54fecf473d3d4069de090657852892063c9910343140c60143e4","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:18.753619Z","id":"19e206cf37315fc102cad83a1eaff23226ba8836955914597c2e71ce17497faf","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Catalog::parse refuses a site domain at, under or above another instance's site domain or public host, and a public host at, under or above another's site domain; test catalog::tests::site_domains_stay_cross_site_from_other_tenants passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"399a7317056084c4affe500bb377380c598e25a707a6787eb4a17d895ac0271f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:18.839194Z","id":"68aceb7ef2bfb3330dccad7d099cf8440de8c17fe615385c851573b628eb0703","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Catalog keeps by_authority, by_site_domain and by_name maps built once per load; resolve looks a site host up by its parent domain. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"d57de89695d4c31d70e425550b0fbcb29edf40ad6c336bb6ef7c0d4cb6d4abb6","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:18.931512Z","id":"55e4815c10dffd7b0dc90894bd0b57e0c8a68096b83cc3c9336961d6816d4c53","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Catalog entries take paused = true: the instance's hosts answer 503, never the default (Fleet::route); docs/kubernetes.md's move and backup procedures use it and say the default catches unknown hosts during a ConfigMap rollout; test leases::a_paused_instance_answers_503_and_stops passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"d20c5ea9e3b99cc4dfd75804d23775c1ba9235515070c85c8add9c034b4e3a4d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.281646Z","id":"53cbc9e34af59bacb9c6b9cf7b02a1dd608d9d2a7aa51779f2facb415255d4bd","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"ROUTE_CACHE is 10 s, forgotten on a 502 or 503 from the target; one lookup per instance runs at a time (Slot::lookup); members are cached from each tick for requests no process forwarded yet. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"a8b682958cdb2402b5c3f709ec28cd7462f4aa03cc6d61c8d889e11a970dec70","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.369950Z","id":"74d8a717e4c3ea5d0550d79c46ea09fed9ce141efc4ec9a455a7e1361b0a54fd","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::handle logs Route::Unavailable's reason and answers a fixed body ('this Clax instance is unavailable; retry'); dispatch no longer echoes the instance header. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"d0041c95f3f85e172667d9feda0f35bc776650dc6e3fdb3cf681945a4086cfa3","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.460231Z","id":"2c98d9412ef245196ff1bf49f6b83aaed1532b3812d34fd1b243c7de066730f2","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Coordinator::release_all frees every lease naming a pod; clax-fleet serve calls Fleet::release_own_leases before its first tick; the contract checks release_all on both coordinators. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"0fc08648dadfc716bcbdc1c301f524816b83a623f27a05167023927828e5144d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.545395Z","id":"61ad76d062a32ab948871657075754077554da3128745b6322637ea0eb41329f","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"fleet.rs place_kek never replaces a home's key: a home holding another key fails to start naming both fingerprints; test fleet::tests::a_home_keeps_its_key_and_its_origin passes; docs/kubernetes.md, CLAX_KEK, says to choose it before first start. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"83a7700da432b79f9f9b191f3721bd8f708a8ff8a60419cb7782b9e08bc65c28","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.624461Z","id":"c708d00ba1af4bfe57cb08b2fcd72d801edf0cf5ceb823afa13756a69184cf58","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Same fix as 83a7700da4: place_kek refuses to overwrite a different key; test fleet::tests::a_home_keeps_its_key_and_its_origin. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"9c3a66f1824a566593070c0623011e3d976e28e42452ffdab1bdb1cdc3a6329c","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.703954Z","id":"3388a264d5bd2e55dffef793f313b0da8f5ddf14d52b439980201fadaa024534","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::open logs when an instance's setup secret is absent ('/setup stays closed; add it to the Secret and restart the pods'); docs/kubernetes.md and deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml say adding or rotating a secret needs kubectl rollout restart. Reading secrets from mounted files is not done. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"2f6f4aabb1af8bf9e0ea28b9a7d85192b524cf4b7e20848561fa0df61292f747","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.782303Z","id":"9d0f4c5c536431dea65f0572ca2ba4d8b682651f14cf7d0302bcab3cf7bd5776","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"FleetConfig::db_limits opens an instance's database with clax_pagevfs::open_with; --db-max-transaction-mib (CLAX_FLEET_DB_MAX_TRANSACTION_MIB) defaults to 256 MiB; docs/kubernetes.md, Sizing, names it. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"440a39279e3d28ace5ceedf2f179d3cc04e4a840b0157db55daa5f8684df3f5b","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.860587Z","id":"62a500fa3316be8e328addf94d50e9956de3630e1e5d668a7a0a64ec09a13dfe","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::stop runs the core's stop on a task of its own and waits at most STOP_TIMEOUT; the slot stays stopping, and Fleet::start refuses it, until that task ends, so a store left running is never opened twice. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"2b58ea4e68c69e4e0e2876bb0b20c29b6a2dc4020ef22861f3aa473a969a21b9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:19.939910Z","id":"c7c2c4796bf1fc4c11f43ccd2ff0c4522435e69e2ee045f0bc7984c9fb3a9719","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::renew_all renews every held lease in one Coordinator::renew_all statement before any stop; renewals and reload no longer wait on a slot a start holds (try_lock); a tick's stops are each bounded by STOP_TIMEOUT. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"23f0a975e228cb86b5b0595803ec2f3c230d43424ccee7bed28ee7480b4785dd","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.018524Z","id":"f6988c9a748757722e0a63f54f8fc2a0396f234abbda99640db79c845f78b6de","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::reload logs a failed reload once per distinct error; /status shows the catalog's loaded_at and error, and /metrics clax_fleet_catalog_stale; Fleet::new's error says the other processes keep the catalog in effect; check-catalog --sqlite reads shards from the catalog, and docs/kubernetes.md shows running it from the image. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"96fb105339b7fdee5422bf5abc5a22302cdc25adf3d3bf50af238a7c642c6b6c","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.097929Z","id":"d54710c67005215042734c9983402da6c6352d8e3742e5d693839ee36faaa5e0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"On SQLite shards a pod starts an instance only when its home is there or its entry says create = true (Fleet::open); test shards::a_shard_without_the_home_does_not_start_an_empty_instance passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"d2e4bf1c2d28a1caca2c6dd74b26ad6bda15aeab22782d34ee90985891c9be79","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.176153Z","id":"d53b3d456aca67ea75e2b6d4f6335e1a1273338c79fbf8fa77288d3f2536a7e4","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"Fleet::start checks draining after taking the slot's lock, so a start either sees the drain or is stopped by it; drain stops every slot. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"6522bc922e83f96d47389641803064670b1afa891e71c2492ab2550aafa8ba80","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.254203Z","id":"8edb416849989190046266d95816b67d87b138190bdf095c96db2690792a5f36","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"The admin port serves /metrics in Prometheus text: requests local/forwarded/refused, forward errors, starts and failures, stops, lease losses and renewal failures, handovers, catalog failures, heartbeat failures, and gauges of running instances, catalog size, staleness and readiness (Fleet::metrics). Pool waits are not counted. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"3e57c16e8984015b4fd72e778b6a339e1820c8c5286cb9c86b51dfc45f1352f4","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.332519Z","id":"8d485b735c13738d42cfab662fa3203375b7c464b93e7afb1f122cdb32d5ae5a","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"/status and /metrics answer only a loopback peer or the peer key (Fleet::admin_router, allowed); /healthz and /readyz stay open for probes; base/networkpolicy.yaml limits 7481. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"7fed13558708928705b3c596bb215826a027ba89423b5ef01a4f59ba2a3232e2","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.410943Z","id":"a47df3f4ed93584a0748289b36a05dc084dc4eefa351a28b6a37e451ac78879b","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"dispatch believes x-clax-fleet-hop and x-clax-fleet-instance only beside x-clax-fleet-key matching the fleet's peer key (constant-time, proxy::key_matches); test leases::a_clients_hop_headers_are_ignored_without_the_peer_key passes. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"462bbaa8851ae571b3e05b01e3960265e7fe21a46f1d62b1966d5f5f9e3d5251","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.490423Z","id":"a7cce4789937b2bcc75e8a2e58e0791d559a3d4acd7034938c9e20dcd2785875","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"CLAX_POSTGRES_POOL defaults to 8, the coordinator has its own 2; docs/kubernetes.md, Connections, gives pods x (pool + 2) against max_connections and PgBouncer; postgres-dev sets max_connections=200. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"16a32d00d46e3cee91159c8cd1634003a6feae5be12cfaeb18cbd90f1b950b42","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.568228Z","id":"0b2c67fb53f2a4efe5a35fb225d27ace41f9c51b8c9ecb391231254f832462db","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json logs JSON lines (tracing-subscriber json); each request runs in an `instance` span (Fleet::handle); RUST_LOG is in docs/kubernetes.md's table. An instance's background tasks are not in a span. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"74d56d45b73b58523464374a4d8ea5d04e46060b0c2ae90c014fbe24a979dd5d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:20.647483Z","id":"f749d8c340bf400b3a6c317317db86bd17c552cc28b18e8ade5c199d969f82c0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"PgCoordinator gets a pool of its own (main.rs COORD_POOL, 2 connections), so heartbeats, renewals and lease lookups never queue behind page and content jobs. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"4a8bd70e5ad33414979db0b47aaf55f53804f11c4b70b34cbee3f950d0249db9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.198808Z","id":"2ac7070bb1b2634d29a6878072dc389ac5af260825c5e49a36658577658865b9","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"proxy::Proxy forwards over a hyper-util pooled client with keep-alive (one pool per owner, 64 idle), resolving names only for new connections; upgrades alone take a connection of their own; proxy tests pass. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"4e75db7e1ba8a4bbbc4c5d7abc3c97765776cd8d2d72e0a91aaa627b0a6d4a2d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.277895Z","id":"21f28cb62f4804e04d12a66331bc2446927f8ea485f818f9c4ad31092c57ccca","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:347050368","summary":"docs/kubernetes.md, Security, says forwards are plain HTTP and to run an encrypted pod network or mTLS mesh; base/networkpolicy.yaml limits who reaches the ports; proxy.rs module docs say so. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-pagevfs/...` all PASSED (integration 17 tests, library 15, binary 3, pagevfs 8)","supersedes":"fc451012515ec2bacae0917337b16d7e031bee0b11685cd30d32faee86be587a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427071Z","id":"922988b532974b7741e44fa7d86ae06fcabb139ea7b1ad11a8ab1ca5a8eedfeb","body":{"detail":"Proxy::new sets only a connect timeout (CONNECT_TIMEOUT, 5 s) and an idle timeout on hyper_util's legacy Client; it sets no response-header timeout and no HttpConnector::set_keepalive. forward() awaits proxy.pool.request(...) (proxy.rs:190) and, for an upgrade, the handshake and send_request (proxy.rs:209-223) with no bound. When an owner's node is lost or partitioned (no FIN, no RST), a request written to a kept-alive pooled connection (reused for up to IDLE_TIMEOUT, 60 s) waits until the kernel gives up retransmitting (about 15 minutes with Linux's default tcp_retries2). Fleet::handle forgets the route only on a 502 or 503 response (fleet.rs:399-407), which never comes, so for ROUTE_CACHE (10 s) every new request for that instance is sent to the dead address too, and the clients' requests and the forwarding pod's tasks pile up although the lease expires after 30 s and another pod could serve the instance.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":123},"end":{"line":135},"content_hash":"906348f656f15e406b6c2528ee0808b8cda18e14d8f9edcf3472c786a108205d"},"suggested_fix":"Set TCP keepalive on the connector (set_keepalive with a short interval) and bound the wait for the owner's response headers (tokio::time::timeout around pool.request and around the upgrade's handshake and send_request, generous enough for Clax's long-poll endpoints), answering 502 and forgetting the route when it passes; event streams stay unbounded because only the headers are timed. Add a test with an owner that accepts and never answers.","summary":"A forward has no timeout past connecting, and pooled connections have no TCP keepalive, so requests sent to an owner whose node vanished hang for minutes","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","performance","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427154Z","id":"d0fd8aa8d5046d0f7ec4884036d4d817bad45041925e390d296cf55c401853e0","body":{"detail":"admin_router's allowed() admits a caller when peer.ip().to_canonical().is_loopback(), meaning 127.0.0.0/8 or ::1. docs/kubernetes.md (Security, Pod network) recommends running the fleet on a mesh with mTLS. With Istio's sidecar in its default REDIRECT interception, the proxy connects to the application from 127.0.0.6 (https://istio.io/latest/blog/2021/upcoming-networking-changes/), so every connection to port 7481, from any pod the mesh and base/networkpolicy.yaml admit, arrives from a loopback address and passes the check, and the peer key is never needed. The docs' promise that /status and /metrics answer only to the pod's loopback or the peer key (docs/kubernetes.md, What clients see) then does not hold.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1083},"end":{"line":1087},"content_hash":"75e31f61d759a6826e5eeee5e0955ed8017e6cfd733e8bbba6bc2f5d4248192d"},"suggested_fix":"Bind a separate loopback-only listener for the unauthenticated admin view (or require the peer key or a dedicated admin token on 7481 always), instead of inferring trust from the peer address; at least document that a sidecar mesh defeats the loopback check and how to exclude port 7481 from interception (traffic.sidecar.istio.io/excludeInboundPorts).","summary":"/status and /metrics trust any caller whose address is loopback, which under a sidecar mesh is every caller","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427199Z","id":"5a51b34f14988b29ceb439b2ca6f73795c95da296aea341876dec4b27d41424a","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml admits port 7481 from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/metrics=true for a Prometheus that scrapes /metrics, but the /metrics handler answers 403 to any non-loopback caller without x-clax-fleet-key. docs/kubernetes.md (Operating it) does not say how a scraper authenticates, and a Prometheus ServiceMonitor or scrape config sets an Authorization header easily but a custom header only in recent versions. The only key accepted is the fleet's peer key (Fleet::is_peer), the same secret that makes dispatch (fleet.rs:1204-1219) believe x-clax-fleet-hop and x-clax-fleet-instance on port 7480, so handing it to the monitoring stack widens who can pass for a fleet process.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1113},"end":{"line":1129},"content_hash":"842d40f5450468694c44f18caae99375adbd1e62ecff18467eff7548f3d578aa"},"suggested_fix":"Accept a separate read-only metrics credential (for example CLAX_FLEET_METRICS_TOKEN as an Authorization: Bearer token), or serve /metrics without authentication since it carries counters only and the NetworkPolicy already limits who reaches 7481; document the scrape configuration in docs/kubernetes.md.","summary":"A Prometheus the NetworkPolicy admits to /metrics gets 403 unless it is given the peer key, which also makes it a trusted forwarder on 7480","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","devops","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427236Z","id":"0d1e30d07e119b8d1310bddf6ab9ece529eaaf8d17fddbc50b63052f7be67d39","body":{"detail":"Fleet::start records nothing when open() fails: it logs at error level, releases the lease and answers 503, and the next request for the instance runs the whole start again. On PostgreSQL each attempt bumps the lease epoch (coord.rs acquire), runs pg::claim_origin, opens the page VFS and reruns migrations (fleet.rs:684-698); on SQLite shards a home missing on its shard (fleet.rs:613-619) or a KEK mismatch (place_kek) fails the same way each time. Requests wait on the slot's lookup and running locks with no bound on how many queue, so an instance with a persistent fault (a failing migration, a wrong CLAX_KEK) turns any client's retries into a steady stream of database work and one error line per request.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":592},"end":{"line":601},"content_hash":"855a94742ddad0b101192337ad4c8ba6044dc371b649786c8f901500ad460e76"},"suggested_fix":"Remember a failed start on the slot (when, and the error) and answer 503 without retrying for a backoff that grows per failure (a few seconds up to a minute), reset on success or on a catalog change for that instance; expose the failure in /status. Add a test that two requests within the backoff make one start attempt.","summary":"An instance that fails to start is started again on every request, with no backoff, so anonymous traffic drives repeated lease takes, database opens and error logs","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","performance","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427269Z","id":"42b00a21d3ce34ff74eddb0165d7a3e3360b3a3ff48b69479022814cafb29674","body":{"detail":"Each instance's [hosted] setup_secret_env names a variable in the one process environment all instances share (fleet.rs open, hosted.setup_open). Catalog::parse checks names, origins and site domains for repeats but not setup_secret_env, and when a config omits it the default is CLAX_SETUP_SECRET for every instance (crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:1291). A catalog with two instances that both omit setup_secret_env, or name the same variable, gives both /setup the same secret: the operator who hands tenant A its setup secret has also handed A the means to take over tenant B's instance before B's owner sets it up. docs/kubernetes.md's example names CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_ACME, but nothing enforces distinct names.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":196},"end":{"line":209},"content_hash":"6453fcc49c6cac0a1ba180cf5c3700bd80cd24147fafcee686d6a643eeccb030"},"suggested_fix":"In Catalog::parse, refuse two instances whose effective setup_secret_env (after the default) is the same, naming both instances; consider also refusing the fleet's own variables (CLAX_KEK, CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY, the database URL) as a setup_secret_env. Add the case to a_bad_catalog_is_refused_naming_what_is_wrong.","summary":"The catalog lets several instances read the same setup secret, so whoever holds one tenant's setup secret can claim another tenant's unclaimed instance","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427298Z","id":"4d874951d338e2426f9920c4f949d84cf672a68b5de1e4e14efae99c6e20e60d","body":{"detail":"The module docs (catalog.rs:26-29) and docs/kubernetes.md (The catalog) say that because no site domain is, is under or is above another instance's hosts, 'each tenant's pages stay cross-site from every other tenant'. The check (at_or_under, label nesting) does not establish that: browsers decide same-site by registrable domain from the Public Suffix List. In the documented example, acme's site domain acme-sites.example.com and any other tenant's origin such as globex.clax.example.com share example.com, so acme's artifact pages are same-site with globex's origin: SameSite cookies do not separate them, and an artifact page can set a Domain=example.com cookie that every tenant's origin receives (docs/hosted/public-suffix.md describes the same risk among artifacts).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":226},"end":{"line":252},"content_hash":"691082016ef4c6d20b9a8e1595a2085f2df5ddf73cf2961929e5f89dbc01bd4c"},"suggested_fix":"Either compare registrable domains (the psl crate) and refuse a site domain that shares one with another instance's public host or site domain unless the site domain is itself a public suffix, or change the docs to state the weaker guarantee (no nesting) and require each tenant's site domain to be its own registrable domain or a PSL entry; change the example so the site domain does not share example.com with the origins.","summary":"The catalog checks that hosts are not nested, but documents that tenants stay cross-site, and its own example puts every tenant in one registrable domain","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","security","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:52.427335Z","id":"c5566b466dc866b81afa99d60726c086bb09c67f17f6bf3ce57b3eb5d6d61cd7","body":{"detail":"Every third tick, Fleet::tick pushes a stop for each running instance whose preferred member is another settled pod, and joins them all at once (fleet.rs:802-808). When the autoscaler adds pods (deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml allows 2 to 10), the instances rendezvous hashing moves to them (half of them when going from 2 to 4) stop on the same tick once the new pods pass coord::SETTLE; their clients reconnect together and every instance cold-starts on the new pods at once (page reads and migration checks against PostgreSQL), while the tick itself waits up to STOP_TIMEOUT for the stops. docs/kubernetes.md (Scaling) slows scale-down for this reason but nothing paces scale-up.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":784},"end":{"line":799},"content_hash":"5a6ce85cb9d814db094562b3148ad577fde3f9ee2061b0bf6a9beb049d20ff0d"},"suggested_fix":"Cap handovers per tick (a constant such as 4 per pod per handover tick, or a fraction of the running instances), leaving the rest for later ticks; count the deferred ones in /metrics.","summary":"A scale-up hands over every instance the new pods are preferred for in the same tick, cutting all their streams at once","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","performance","devops","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772789Z","id":"ad7b26f31a4ee3b39e16beb15aff3f2b51d8f900783c6f0b2deead1435dfaf2d","body":{"detail":"Fleet::status (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs, \"commit\": clax_core::build_commit()) reads a OnceLock that only clax_core::set_build_commit fills; the clax binary fills it through clax-commands (crates/clax-commands/src/lib.rs:72) from crates/clax-cli/build.rs's CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT. clax-fleet has no build script and main() never calls set_build_commit (`grep -rn set_build_commit crates` finds no clax-fleet hit). The image build could not supply one anyway: .dockerignore:6 leaves out .git and deploy/Dockerfile passes no CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT. docs/kubernetes.md:341-343 promises /status gives \"its version and commit\", which an operator needs during a rollout of images tagged by commit (fleet-image.yml's type=sha).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":221},"end":{"line":233},"content_hash":"2f28d44a181fae95b6715bc5a6424844783b3d302ce6a6d828a632bf056026f4"},"suggested_fix":"Give clax-fleet a build.rs that sets CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT as crates/clax-cli/build.rs does and call clax_core::set_build_commit(env!(\"CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT\")) in main; add `ARG CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT` to the build stage and pass `build-args: CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT=${{ github.sha }}` in .github/workflows/fleet-image.yml.","summary":"clax-fleet never records its build commit, so /status always reports \"commit\": \"unknown\"","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772882Z","id":"b0081557f5efda61823d9080d42793e70b1738766fa0849d15382b591b1dc731","body":{"detail":"The loop never checks the response status: a 403 (wrong CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY, fleet.rs admin_router) or a 5xx body fails to parse, `unwrap_or_default` yields Null, and the line reads \"shard 1 (?): ready null, running null\", which looks like a live shard. The hyper client has no connect or request timeout, so a shard whose pod IP drops packets holds the command for the OS connect timeout, shard after shard.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":506},"end":{"line":525},"content_hash":"86744a8a941915e70aa06de9224dccc8338128d76d9e2725b7529fcb614078b2"},"suggested_fix":"Print the HTTP status when it is not 200 (\"refused: 403, check CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY\"), and wrap each request in tokio::time::timeout (clax_fleet::proxy::CONNECT_TIMEOUT is 5 s), querying the shards concurrently.","summary":"`clax-fleet status --backend sqlite` prints a refused or failed shard as \"ready null, running null\" and can hang on an unreachable one","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772907Z","id":"0fac0089023241a46b7c3b14e01f794347f82d0de406cd1e24ae00cf28a964fd","body":{"detail":"The query's `m.seen >= now() - interval '20 seconds'` duplicates coord::MEMBER_TTL (crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:30). If the TTL changes, status marks live holders as \"(pod not seen)\" or hides dead ones, and the SQL lives outside the coordinator that owns the schema.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":449},"end":{"line":455},"content_hash":"d372f1c703397edbe4b49a6a22f7df45f6519a16b5c5cd8a3616013e92e7b797"},"suggested_fix":"Bind MEMBER_TTL as a parameter (`now() - make_interval(secs => $1)`), or move the query into clax_fleet::coord as a function the CLI calls.","summary":"`clax-fleet status` hard-codes a 20-second member window instead of using clax_fleet::coord::MEMBER_TTL","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:06:57.772931Z","id":"5185bff9c66de80add196ef498b6a032db26577c76566f545b362b3820ff4782","body":{"detail":"main() sets up a JSON subscriber when asked (main.rs:225-232) but reports the error serve() returns (a bad catalog, CLAX_KEK missing, PostgreSQL unreachable) as plain `clax-fleet: ...` on stderr. A log pipeline that parses JSON lines drops or misfiles it, and it is the line an operator looks for when a pod crash-loops. The module docs (main.rs:4-8) also name the flag `--drain-delay`; clap derives `--drain-delay-secs` from the field (main.rs:186-187), as docs/kubernetes.md:378 says.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":238},"end":{"line":243},"content_hash":"fcff2e64e283ad41ae1dec30c7c83fc73a5c946c62bbf03cfe9476ba9d8e84f6"},"suggested_fix":"Log the error with tracing::error!(error = %format!(\"{e:#}\"), \"clax-fleet failed\") before exiting (keeping eprintln for check-catalog, whose output a person reads), and correct the flag's name in the module docs.","summary":"A fatal error is printed with eprintln even when CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json, so the one line explaining a crash loop is not JSON","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.817726Z","id":"b81c6ae19227fe4698ba2f47b06a27c207c02db822fed30d76558dc0ebae8b79","body":{"detail":"At bac1e8702, crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs:123-135 (Proxy::new) sets only set_connect_timeout(CONNECT_TIMEOUT), set_nodelay, pool_idle_timeout(IDLE_TIMEOUT=60s, proxy.rs:102), pool_max_idle_per_host; `grep -n 'keepalive\\|timeout' proxy.rs` shows no keepalive and no other timeout except the upgrade connect (proxy.rs:203). proxy.pool.request(...).await (proxy.rs:190), handshake (proxy.rs:209) and send_request (proxy.rs:219) are unbounded. Fleet::handle forgets the route only on 502/503 (fleet.rs:399-407); cached_route keeps the address for ROUTE_CACHE=10s (fleet.rs:88, 355-358). main.rs and fleet.rs add no request timeout layer (grep -in timeout: only CONNECTIONS_DRAIN and STOP_TIMEOUT). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"922988b532974b7741e44fa7d86ae06fcabb139ea7b1ad11a8ab1ca5a8eedfeb","summary":"confirmed: forwards have no timeout past connect and pooled connections no TCP keepalive","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818006Z","id":"47c30e26e264334d167b836c403ab304701612eb53519f1b0eb0ba6390e2f07e","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1085-1087: allowed() is `peer.ip().to_canonical().is_loopback() || f.is_peer(headers)`, guarding /status (fleet.rs:1101-1111) and /metrics (1113-1129); the admin port listens on 0.0.0.0:7481 (docs/kubernetes.md:365). docs/kubernetes.md:263 recommends a mesh with mTLS, and docs/kubernetes.md:275-277 promises /status and /metrics answer only to loopback or the peer key. Istio's sidecar delivers inbound traffic to the app from 127.0.0.6 (https://istio.io/latest/blog/2021/upcoming-networking-changes/), which is in 127.0.0.0/8, so the check passes for every caller the NetworkPolicy (deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml, port 7481 rule) admits. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"d0fd8aa8d5046d0f7ec4884036d4d817bad45041925e390d296cf55c401853e0","summary":"confirmed: admin allowed() trusts any loopback peer address, which a sidecar mesh makes every caller","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818249Z","id":"89f5dd810ea016ad7ace6b37dcd441bfbf14e26c9525486b71cf99d7f58d93c5","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml admits 7481 from namespaces labelled clax.empathic.dev/metrics=true 'for a Prometheus that scrapes /metrics'. crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1113-1129 answers 403 unless allowed() (fleet.rs:1085-1087: loopback or Fleet::is_peer, the x-clax-fleet-key peer key). docs/kubernetes.md:338-346 (Operating it) and 275-277 name no scrape credential. The same is_peer gates believing x-clax-fleet-hop / x-clax-fleet-instance in dispatch (fleet.rs:1199-1219), so the only working credential also makes the scraper a trusted forwarder on 7480. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"5a51b34f14988b29ceb439b2ca6f73795c95da296aea341876dec4b27d41424a","summary":"confirmed: a Prometheus admitted to 7481 gets 403 on /metrics unless given the peer key","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818484Z","id":"231061025e0a5bbb85f65b1abec9a96db958fc82972791018cf0e6041db9f51f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:535-601 (Fleet::start): on open() Err it bumps start_failures, logs tracing::error!, loses the fence, releases the lease and returns Unavailable; nothing is stored on the slot (`*running` stays None), so the next caller holding slot.running (fleet.rs:537) re-runs coord.acquire (561-563) and open(). open() (fleet.rs:606-698) on PostgreSQL runs pg::claim_origin, clax_pagevfs::open_with and Store::open_on (migrations) in spawn_blocking each time; on shards a missing home bails at fleet.rs:613-619 and place_kek refuses a mismatched KEK (fleet.rs:625-627, 1133+). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"0d1e30d07e119b8d1310bddf6ab9ece529eaaf8d17fddbc50b63052f7be67d39","summary":"confirmed: a failed start is retried in full on every request, with no backoff or remembered failure","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818721Z","id":"c65adf525f2165cd0b0bac72eee5c818bf4cea5f7d7eb3da9e97802fd01e7a9b","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-core/src/config.rs:729-731 defaults setup_secret_env to SETUP_SECRET_ENV = \"CLAX_SETUP_SECRET\" (config.rs:1290-1291). `grep -n setup_secret crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs` returns nothing: Catalog::parse (catalog.rs:196-250) checks repeated public origins (authorities set) and site-domain nesting, not setup_secret_env. fleet.rs open() reads each instance's variable from the one process environment (hosted.setup_open(), fleet.rs:641-647). docs/kubernetes.md:108 uses CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_ACME by convention only. Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"42b00a21d3ce34ff74eddb0165d7a3e3360b3a3ff48b69479022814cafb29674","summary":"confirmed: Catalog::parse lets instances share a setup_secret_env, defaulting all to CLAX_SETUP_SECRET","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.818952Z","id":"34e910382b99c1ccc53f27d427d6d626eb4bca1fd37f415d8eb6e6a0f25e4845","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs:26-29 and docs/kubernetes.md:116 state 'each tenant's pages stay cross-site from every other tenant'; the check at catalog.rs:226-250 is at_or_under label nesting only. docs/kubernetes.md:106-107 pairs public_origin acme.clax.example.com with site_domain acme-sites.example.com: both have registrable domain example.com under the Public Suffix List (https://publicsuffix.org/), so they are same-site, which docs/hosted/public-suffix.md:1-20 describes (Domain= cookies, SameSite not separating). Concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"4d874951d338e2426f9920c4f949d84cf672a68b5de1e4e14efae99c6e20e60d","summary":"confirmed: the nesting check does not make tenants cross-site, and the documented example shares example.com","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:48.819187Z","id":"c48f152be47c84b011e8a7ea461eb1555ff7a90542f021d33129562f368213f2","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:784-799: on n % 3 == 2 every running instance whose coord::preferred member is another pod aged >= coord::SETTLE is pushed to stops, with no cap; fleet.rs:802-808 joins all stops with futures::future::join_all, each bounded by STOP_TIMEOUT (fleet.rs:91, 753). deploy/k8s/postgres/hpa.yaml:13-14 allows 2 to 10 replicas. docs/kubernetes.md:228-234 slows scale-down because 'each move cuts that instance's open streams' but describes no pacing for scale-up. Suggestion is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"c5566b466dc866b81afa99d60726c086bb09c67f17f6bf3ce57b3eb5d6d61cd7","summary":"confirmed: a scale-up hands over every preferred instance in one tick, unpaced","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:14.037559Z","id":"79767583f0d4de8326f5cf0b86d503b9e692554adc6355146293e97fa3526113","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:979 `\"commit\": clax_core::build_commit()`; crates/clax-core/src/lib.rs:61-71 returns \"unknown\" until set_build_commit fills the OnceLock. `grep -rn 'set_build_commit' crates` (excluding .qual) hits only crates/clax-worker/src/instance.rs:668, crates/clax-commands/src/lib.rs:72 (inside clax_commands::run, which clax-fleet never calls) and clax-core's definition. crates/clax-fleet has no build.rs (ls: BUILD.bazel Cargo.toml src tests), main() (main.rs:221-243) never sets it, .dockerignore excludes .git, and deploy/Dockerfile passes no CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT. docs/kubernetes.md:341-342 promises \"its version and commit\".","kind":"comment","references":"ad7b26f31a4ee3b39e16beb15aff3f2b51d8f900783c6f0b2deead1435dfaf2d","summary":"confirmed: nothing in clax-fleet calls set_build_commit, so /status's commit is always \"unknown\"","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:41.331370Z","id":"84adc9c1a0c2d9d697e503fb1ed06c09aaf9433b57a724db7319a36749f028d4","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:499-525: client built with `Client::builder(TokioExecutor::new()).build_http()` (no connect or request timeout); on Ok(resp) it parses the body with `serde_json::from_slice(&body).unwrap_or_default()` without checking resp.status(), then prints `shard {shard} ({pod or \"?\"}): ready {v[\"ready\"]}, running {v[\"running\"]}`. The 403 from fleet.rs:1107/1120 (`StatusCode::FORBIDDEN.into_response()`, empty body) parses to Null, printing \"ready null, running null\".","kind":"comment","references":"b0081557f5efda61823d9080d42793e70b1738766fa0849d15382b591b1dc731","summary":"confirmed: the SQLite status loop ignores the response status and the client has no timeout","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:41.331659Z","id":"e2a9f0b637124dee9044739fb398c80a82016d289a21acd7de1d1470da398867","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:453 `ON m.pod = l.holder AND m.seen >= now() - interval '20 seconds'`; crates/clax-fleet/src/coord.rs:30 `pub const MEMBER_TTL: Duration = Duration::from_secs(20);`. The two agree today but nothing ties them.","kind":"comment","references":"0fac0089023241a46b7c3b14e01f794347f82d0de406cd1e24ae00cf28a964fd","summary":"confirmed: status hard-codes interval '20 seconds', duplicating coord::MEMBER_TTL","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:41.331907Z","id":"baa36d301732a1922fb13eab7ca990c10fc14adfec03e4918abfc345e1973338","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:225-232 installs a JSON subscriber for LogFormat::Json; main.rs:238-241 handles Err(e) with `eprintln!(\"clax-fleet: {e:#}\")`, not tracing. main.rs:6 says `--drain-delay`, while the field is `drain_delay_secs` (main.rs:186-187, clap long derives --drain-delay-secs), matching docs/kubernetes.md:378 `--drain-delay-secs`.","kind":"comment","references":"5185bff9c66de80add196ef498b6a032db26577c76566f545b362b3820ff4782","summary":"confirmed: the fatal error goes through eprintln regardless of --log-format json, and the module docs name --drain-delay","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.410116Z","id":"0359b0167259d2f71dc58ed58e33aae330c2bcb66cc480f45e033ce2628d15dc","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Catalog::parse refuses two instances with the same effective setup_secret_env (the default CLAX_SETUP_SECRET included), naming both, and refuses CLAX_KEK, CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY and CLAX_POSTGRES_URL as one. Test: catalog a_bad_catalog_is_refused_naming_what_is_wrong. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"42b00a21d3ce34ff74eddb0165d7a3e3360b3a3ff48b69479022814cafb29674","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.493242Z","id":"e362b14a8b62fb2aa8cefb0fdb0d9c229ff0f86899bd60f14941dbb1eea6829d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"No public-suffix crate is in Cargo.lock, so the catalog module docs and docs/kubernetes.md now state the weaker guarantee (no nesting) and require each tenant's site domain to be its own registrable domain or a PSL entry; the example site domain is acme-sites.example.net. Test renamed no_site_domain_is_at_another_tenants_hosts. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"4d874951d338e2426f9920c4f949d84cf672a68b5de1e4e14efae99c6e20e60d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:39.981073Z","id":"8d3dc4918d752f115fe8ede5aba51509f3429358cf7d81b9dc976eb5f3ea2326","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"A failed open is recorded on the slot; requests answer 503 without a new start until a backoff (2 s doubling to 60 s) passes, reset on success or a catalog change; /status lists failing instances. Time comes from FleetConfig.clock. Test: shards::a_failed_start_is_retried_after_a_backoff_not_per_request. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"0d1e30d07e119b8d1310bddf6ab9ece529eaaf8d17fddbc50b63052f7be67d39","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.061828Z","id":"1eeb216ccdb0bd8a59bdf23c190fbef68bf7110aa5eb36ccedd606c91a71cebf","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"The handover moves at most HANDOVERS_PER_TICK (4) instances per tick and counts the rest in clax_fleet_handovers_deferred_total. Test: leases::a_handover_moves_a_few_instances_per_tick. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"c5566b466dc866b81afa99d60726c086bb09c67f17f6bf3ce57b3eb5d6d61cd7","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.142680Z","id":"2e976c8fa12c657c6a014f96bd6bf26e80a04d40f1dc16a5b178d4e3e1ca2005","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Port 7481 no longer trusts loopback peers: /status there needs the peer key. A loopback-only listener (CLAX_FLEET_LOCAL_ADMIN_LISTEN, default 127.0.0.1:7482, non-loopback refused) serves /status open. Test: shards::the_admin_port_asks_for_the_key_for_status_alone. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"d0fd8aa8d5046d0f7ec4884036d4d817bad45041925e390d296cf55c401853e0","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.223600Z","id":"116228b8d7f3d7ea3f051bba0cad42dd48862936b28edb794fab98d4cce7128a","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"/metrics asks for no credential (counters and gauges only; base/networkpolicy.yaml limits who reaches 7481), so a scraper never needs the peer key; docs/kubernetes.md gains a Scraping /metrics part with a PodMonitor. Test: shards::the_admin_port_asks_for_the_key_for_status_alone. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"5a51b34f14988b29ceb439b2ca6f73795c95da296aea341876dec4b27d41424a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.305053Z","id":"5ded22dd82933664ef1f261ba1a83a239aac68cbb471f04367122b04bf427164","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"crates/clax-fleet/build.rs reads CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT (falling back to unknown) and main() calls clax_core::set_build_commit; deploy/Dockerfile takes ARG CLAX_BUILD_COMMIT and fleet-image.yml passes github.sha. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"ad7b26f31a4ee3b39e16beb15aff3f2b51d8f900783c6f0b2deead1435dfaf2d","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.386745Z","id":"2b15174ba837291bc0a32649231e976f5aa2236f9250ef888a705a69bdc4ed2f","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"serve's fatal error goes through tracing::error! when logging JSON; the module docs name --drain-delay-secs. Test: tests::only_serve_logging_json_logs_its_fatal_error. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"5185bff9c66de80add196ef498b6a032db26577c76566f545b362b3820ff4782","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.468574Z","id":"455eaed7875c6dbcc0c7bdb5ab3e99616cdc1a1958b8870bcacd68b3e4860def","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"The status query binds coord::MEMBER_TTL (now() - make_interval(secs => $1)). Compiles; not run without PostgreSQL. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"0fac0089023241a46b7c3b14e01f794347f82d0de406cd1e24ae00cf28a964fd","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:40.550174Z","id":"451bcd0f33762dc2f6db22c35b96cac82685269dd5a16d2d6f4204d50fe1b4fb","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"clax-fleet status --backend sqlite asks the shards concurrently with a 5 s timeout, and says when a shard refuses (403, check CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY), answers another status, sends no JSON or does not answer. Test: tests::a_shard_that_refuses_or_does_not_answer_says_so. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"b0081557f5efda61823d9080d42793e70b1738766fa0849d15382b591b1dc731","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:41.399949Z","id":"2c1910b7e224c03fe15d2eb15b63469faab0ed9b9dd3d419589493a83e6e4194","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Pooled forwards get TCP keepalive and, on Linux, TCP_USER_TIMEOUT 30 s; response headers and an upgrade's handshake are bounded by RESPONSE_TIMEOUT (61 min, above Clax's 3600 s long polls), answering 502. Test: proxy::an_owner_that_never_answers_is_502 (plain and WebSocket). Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"922988b532974b7741e44fa7d86ae06fcabb139ea7b1ad11a8ab1ca5a8eedfeb","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T04:34:54.799579Z","id":"3b33204dfa8f3425e9d186ef101495679b4e89b59d2e11d46e75071fc2bce420","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Obsolete: the PostgreSQL backend is gone from clax-fleet (fleet.rs has no Storage::Postgres; every instance opens its home with Core::start, the reader pool included).","supersedes":"6fcfa36bf488619218d5e358e395168e30546c4f01f47395ba0f9c69df176c68","tags":["reason:obsolete","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T04:34:54.891774Z","id":"4036220efd5f2793eff457b1b917238801d2dcdc4b677d04fbd1975c91b8cf2d","body":{"kind":"resolve","summary":"Obsolete: the PostgreSQL backend is gone from clax-fleet; an instance's home is on its shard's volume, where clax serve's copy of a database before a migration is kept (docs/kubernetes.md, Upgrading).","supersedes":"73581afdd3b3b90fdd863d950c1f025dd39a802adddfdebf4d1f232c080ee5ca","tags":["reason:obsolete","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-29T04:34:55.065963Z","id":"37aca9b63fb9b71f34b91c40dc9f751c1d885e7b5087ac7e2a844250337fec98","body":{"detail":"Every instance's home is on its shard pod's volume, so a pod that is down takes its shard's instances down until it returns. Two ways to keep the data elsewhere were weighed. (1) SQLite pages in PostgreSQL: the store's database as chunks through a custom VFS, instances placed by leases and every write fenced by the lease epoch. It was built on this branch and removed: it made clax-fleet own a storage engine layer (atomic chunk commits, unknown sizes after a lost reply, a VFS under SQLite's extern \"C\" callbacks) and distributed ownership (leases, fences, handover, forwarding by lease), whose failure paths were hard to test, to store opaque pages in a relational database. (2) A native PostgreSQL backend for crates/clax-store: broad but shallow work (its queries and migrations in a second dialect, json_each and rowid uses, update_hook-driven events as LISTEN/NOTIFY or emitted by the store, per-artifact app data stores as schemas or keyed tables), with PostgreSQL itself providing concurrency, durability and failover. If failover is needed, take (2), or first measure replicating each volume (Litestream to object storage) against it.","kind":"alternative","span":{"start":{"line":17},"end":{"line":25},"content_hash":"b3a0cc050cc3fb84bb5383f737a3294e8096d538192de3c39ef523368f138513"},"summary":"Keep instances' data off the pods, in PostgreSQL, so any pod can run any instance and a lost pod's instances fail over","tags":["revisit:an instance must survive the loss of its pod without waiting for the pod to return","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994594Z","id":"b43ac9eeea5019cb94dd9ce823d29173e619651bb501dfff7501ea2de5ec4262","body":{"detail":"peer_key() (main.rs:230-236) rejects only an empty or all-whitespace key and returns it untrimmed. Fleet::new then calls Proxy::new (proxy.rs:168), which does `HeaderValue::from_str(key).expect(\"the peer key is a header value\")`; HeaderValue rejects control bytes such as `\\n`. `openssl rand -hex 32 > key && kubectl create secret generic clax-secrets --from-file=CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY=key` is a common way to make the Secret, and the file ends in a newline, so every pod crash-loops with a Rust panic whose text names neither CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY nor the fix. The panic goes to stderr as plain text even under CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json (no panic hook routes it through tracing), so a JSON log pipeline drops the one line that explains it. kek_from_env (main.rs:215) already trims CLAX_KEK; the peer key gets no such treatment.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":230},"end":{"line":236},"content_hash":"b2af2ba3e425253701c2b431152166ab4f26d298b2c40df56a646bea195a49df"},"suggested_fix":"In peer_key(), trim the key and check it is a valid header value (visible ASCII), failing with `CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY holds a character a header cannot carry (a trailing newline from --from-file?)`; make Proxy::new take an already-validated key so it cannot panic. Optionally install a panic hook that logs through tracing when the format is json.","summary":"A CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY ending in a newline (a Secret made --from-file) passes the startup check and then panics in Proxy::new without naming the variable","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994660Z","id":"03cf281ef1d52c49967f75567bb8244a25ab1f94ba1bec5e2f0e570234776d0b","body":{"detail":"LogFormat::Text calls `tracing_subscriber::fmt().with_env_filter(filter).init()`. tracing-subscriber 0.3.23 (Cargo.lock) is built with its default `ansi` feature (the workspace's `tracing-subscriber = { version = \"0.3\", features = [\"env-filter\"] }` in Cargo.toml does not turn defaults off), and its fmt layer emits ANSI colour codes unless NO_COLOR is set; it does not check whether stderr is a terminal. In a pod stdout is a pipe to the container runtime, so `kubectl logs` and Loki/Fluent Bit/CloudWatch store lines like `\\x1b[2m2026-...\\x1b[0m \\x1b[32m INFO\\x1b[0m`, which breaks grep and level parsing. Text is the default (main.rs:39-45) and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml sets no CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT, so this is what an operator following docs/kubernetes.md gets.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":154},"end":{"line":163},"content_hash":"1e61dc2d229fe2e7245e60a9488be0b09366b69a88a780de4e4cdc6f254afafa"},"suggested_fix":"Call `.with_ansi(std::io::IsTerminal::is_terminal(&std::io::stderr()))` (and write to stderr explicitly) on the text formatter, so colour appears only on a terminal; consider making json the default when stdout is not a terminal.","summary":"Text logs, the default, carry ANSI colour escapes into kubectl logs and cluster log collectors","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994698Z","id":"de5c026dae27c8a7a849d460b9edcb12f00f944c38324c1c0753d0c32b630589","body":{"detail":"`EnvFilter::try_from_default_env().unwrap_or_else(|_| EnvFilter::new(\"info\"))` discards the parse error as well as the unset case. An operator who sets RUST_LOG=clax_fleet=degub (or a directive with a typo in its target syntax) to chase a problem gets info-level logs and no word that their setting was ignored, while every other bad setting (CLAX_KEK, the peer key, the catalog) fails fast with a message naming it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":154},"end":{"line":155},"content_hash":"91ca7cd869aedef031457d347b28e5dadb78d1b5ac786b1918e4216928bc5b06"},"suggested_fix":"Distinguish the cases: when RUST_LOG is set but does not parse, either fail with `RUST_LOG: <error>` or log a warning naming the bad value right after the subscriber is installed with the fallback filter.","summary":"An unparseable RUST_LOG is silently replaced by `info`","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994730Z","id":"b5fc05c9c0ecd94ad619721a661c08b9df5124c463be64ebff4ff0b24c564438","body":{"detail":"`a.ordinal.or_else(|| ordinal_of(&pod))` takes the flag or env whenever present. Env in a StatefulSet's pod template is the same for every replica, so setting CLAX_FLEET_ORDINAL there (it is listed as a plain variable in docs/kubernetes.md's table, \"this pod's shard\") gives clax-0 and clax-1 the same ordinal. Each then treats that shard's instances as its own (fleet.rs:368-373): an instance with `create = true` gets a fresh home on the second pod's volume (fleet.rs:510-517), open to /setup again, and the Service splits that tenant's requests between two divergent copies. Nothing logs that the explicit ordinal disagrees with the pod name, and no shard detects another process advertising its ordinal.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":264},"end":{"line":266},"content_hash":"9853761caa6d6c9f54513032f8482738a79994d4a271d12cc3679bedf19f6eb0"},"suggested_fix":"When both an explicit ordinal and a pod-name ordinal exist and differ, fail at startup naming both (or require an explicit `--ordinal-overrides-pod-name` style opt-in); at minimum log a warning. Document the variable as for non-StatefulSet runs only.","summary":"CLAX_FLEET_ORDINAL overrides the pod name's ordinal without any cross-check, so one value in a StatefulSet's pod template makes every pod claim the same shard and create duplicate homes","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994758Z","id":"d3dc7c58822e51a609d5b4e57142a39ce104eb535fd8eb40c4a175580fb46b83","body":{"detail":"CLAX_FLEET_PEER defaults to `clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480` and Peers::addr (fleet.rs:96-101) only does `replace(\"{shard}\", ..)`. A template without `{shard}` sends every shard's forwards to one host, where they come back or are refused 503 after MAX_HOPS. A StatefulSet renamed by a kustomize namePrefix (pods `prod-clax-0`), or a headless Service with another name, leaves the default pointing at names that do not resolve: every request for another shard's instance answers 502 and logs a per-request warning (proxy.rs:117-118), while /readyz stays 200 and startup says nothing. serve() already knows the pod name and its own ordinal, so it can compare `peers.addr(ordinal)`'s host with the pod name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":109},"end":{"line":115},"content_hash":"862dcbbc43e30cfe285440bc05db07c3af1e22f1669d714c4439e39ba6042159"},"suggested_fix":"At startup, fail when the template lacks `{shard}`, and warn (or fail) when the first DNS label of `peers.addr(ordinal)` is not the pod name, naming CLAX_FLEET_PEER and the expected form; optionally resolve the own peer address once and log when it does not reach this pod.","summary":"The peer template is never validated: one without `{shard}`, or whose host does not match the StatefulSet's pod names, makes every cross-shard forward fail at request time with no startup warning","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994803Z","id":"855134a1d21ad40050eddfcf4703cdf9501baf2fb4e1f923c1473b04d47591ff","body":{"detail":"stop() takes `slot.running.lock().await` before STOP_TIMEOUT applies; the timeout wraps only the spawned core.stop() (fleet.rs:596). start() holds that same lock across `self.open(inst).await` (fleet.rs:386-411), which runs Core::start and the store's migrations. drain() stops every slot (fleet.rs:673-686), so one start in progress at SIGTERM holds the whole drain for as long as it takes. The budget docs/kubernetes.md (Draining) and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:23-26 give is drain delay 5 s + stops 10 s + connections 10 s within a 45 s grace period; a start past about 20 s is SIGKILLed with its store open, and the log never says the drain was waiting on a start.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":573},"end":{"line":602},"content_hash":"9b241458c57761458e2955cf590d995152d736640b9ebe979879be393994db1f"},"suggested_fix":"Bound the lock wait in stop() by the same STOP_TIMEOUT (`tokio::time::timeout(STOP_TIMEOUT, slot.running.lock())`), logging `waiting on a start of <instance>` when it expires, or make drain() measure one overall deadline derived from a configurable grace budget and log what it abandoned.","summary":"A drain waits without limit for an instance start under way, so SIGTERM during a slow start (a long migration) can outlast terminationGracePeriodSeconds and end in SIGKILL","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994841Z","id":"0d944b7e9e7b30a3c4b18e7ce642b5d6dad5915aeeb1584046a1decf1c181091","body":{"detail":"Instances start lazily and a failed start backs off (fleet.rs:425-451); the only signal is the counter clax_fleet_instance_start_failures_total, which a rate alert sees only while requests keep arriving. /readyz answers ready whenever the process is not draining (fleet.rs:297-300), and /status, which lists `failing`, needs the peer key that Prometheus is never given (deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml's comment). Version and commit are only in /status, so `which pods run the new image` is not answerable from metrics during a RollingUpdate.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":766},"end":{"line":778},"content_hash":"d7def346415bdd77569ddc01b4317aaa03d5623a9377f3a3583818c1289dd174"},"suggested_fix":"Add a gauge `clax_fleet_instances_failing` (slots whose `failed` is set, no names) and `clax_fleet_build_info{version=\"..\",commit=\"..\"} 1`; optionally `clax_fleet_shard{ordinal=\"..\"}` and `clax_fleet_catalog_shards` so an alert can catch ordinal >= shards (now only the warning in check_ordinal, fleet.rs:266-276).","summary":"/metrics has no gauge of instances failing to start and no build-info series, so an operator cannot alert on a shard whose instances are all down or see a rollout's progress","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:35:25.994873Z","id":"99927e7f695b60186b811a1860d94812f762e9452657a0d3c2078c5139198643","body":{"detail":"The three `TcpListener::bind(..).await?` calls propagate the io::Error as is, so the pod's last line is `clax-fleet: Address already in use (os error 98)` or `Permission denied (os error 13)` (a port under 1024 with capabilities dropped, as deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml does), without saying whether CLAX_FLEET_LISTEN, CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN or CLAX_FLEET_LOCAL_ADMIN_LISTEN is at fault. Every other startup error in serve() names its setting.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":313},"end":{"line":315},"content_hash":"51db218f53c9c7c1dbb5e093b5423a2c38ba40cf02f5e02c4d4acf7d6538d1d9"},"suggested_fix":"Wrap each bind with anyhow context naming the flag, variable and address, e.g. `.with_context(|| format!(\"--admin-listen (CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN) {admin_listen}\"))`.","summary":"A listen address that cannot be bound fails with the OS error alone, not the flag or variable that set it","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.354513Z","id":"0fd5bc3a7c0d0f4cb71381d68b3287030de59d8a140d7b616f563a7d8a5a8e16","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:230-236 peer_key() filters only `k.trim().is_empty()` and returns the original string; main.rs:267 passes it to FleetConfig; crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:247 calls Proxy::new(&cfg.peer_key); crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs:168 is `HeaderValue::from_str(key).expect(\"the peer key is a header value\")`, and HeaderValue::from_str rejects control bytes such as `\\n` (https://docs.rs/http/latest/http/header/struct.HeaderValue.html#method.from_str). `grep -rn set_hook crates/clax-fleet` finds no panic hook, so under CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT=json the panic is plain stderr, outside the json error path of main.rs:170-180. main.rs:215 trims CLAX_KEK; the peer key is not trimmed. Kind concern fits.","kind":"comment","references":"b43ac9eeea5019cb94dd9ce823d29173e619651bb501dfff7501ea2de5ec4262","summary":"confirmed: an untrimmed peer key with a trailing newline reaches HeaderValue::from_str(..).expect and panics","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.355496Z","id":"47115f41878e3c13667ed64ce4753ce020ed458c562451a4653099a790630f51","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:157 is `tracing_subscriber::fmt().with_env_filter(filter).init()` with no with_ansi. Cargo.toml:117 keeps tracing-subscriber's default features (which include `ansi`), Cargo.lock:3848-3849 pins 0.3.23, whose src/fmt/fmt_layer.rs:740-743 sets `let ansi = cfg!(feature = \"ansi\") && env::var(\"NO_COLOR\").map_or(true, |v| v.is_empty());` with no terminal check, writing to io::stdout (line 748). main.rs:39-45 defaults to text and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:57-71 sets neither CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT nor NO_COLOR. One detail: the writer is stdout, not stderr; the suggested fix should test stdout's terminal-ness (or switch the writer).","kind":"comment","references":"03cf281ef1d52c49967f75567bb8244a25ab1f94ba1bec5e2f0e570234776d0b","summary":"confirmed: the default text formatter emits ANSI escapes whenever NO_COLOR is unset, terminal or not","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.356397Z","id":"fc26dc7c1400b34ad378fd01df7aa25759d393ee3ee30d0ad410757be5885c73","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:154-155 `EnvFilter::try_from_default_env().unwrap_or_else(|_| EnvFilter::new(\"info\"))`: try_from_default_env returns Err both for an unset variable and for a parse error (https://docs.rs/tracing-subscriber/0.3.23/tracing_subscriber/filter/struct.EnvFilter.html#method.try_from_default_env), and the closure discards it. Nothing after line 163 logs it. Kind suggestion fits.","kind":"comment","references":"de5c026dae27c8a7a849d460b9edcb12f00f944c38324c1c0753d0c32b630589","summary":"confirmed: a RUST_LOG that fails to parse falls back to info with no message","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.357309Z","id":"4efde83c392b7b279a048e9b9393b068d7a03d1c2e0ee7c5abf70f9719046f5a","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:264 `a.ordinal.or_else(|| ordinal_of(&pod))`; main.rs:107 reads CLAX_FLEET_ORDINAL; nothing compares it with ordinal_of(&pod). crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:368-373 starts any instance whose shard equals the ordinal, and fleet.rs:510-517 makes a fresh home when `inst.create` is set and none exists. fleet.rs:266-276 check_ordinal checks only ordinal >= shards. docs/kubernetes.md:348 lists the variable as `this pod's shard`. Kind concern fits (a misconfiguration risk; the shipped manifests do not set it).","kind":"comment","references":"b5fc05c9c0ecd94ad619721a661c08b9df5124c463be64ebff4ff0b24c564438","summary":"confirmed: an explicit ordinal wins over the pod name with no comparison, so a template-wide value gives every pod one shard","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.358223Z","id":"cabfe53907f27e40d2df55021a9de5813f6f3a1ee5b657ebe0485a541e2f7f8f","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:110-115 takes CLAX_FLEET_PEER as a String and main.rs:277 wraps it in Peers::Template unchecked; crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:96-101 Peers::addr is `t.replace(\"{shard}\", ..)`. deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:58-59 hardcodes `clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480` as an env value, which a kustomize namePrefix does not rewrite (it renames the StatefulSet and Service, not env strings). Forward failures answer 502 per request (crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs:117-118) while Fleet::ready (fleet.rs:297-300) depends only on draining. Kind concern fits.","kind":"comment","references":"d3dc7c58822e51a609d5b4e57142a39ce104eb535fd8eb40c4a175580fb46b83","summary":"confirmed: the peer template is used as given; nothing at startup checks `{shard}` or the host against the pod name","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.359078Z","id":"a304f12c8ef066122fd00ab7ff2c5f48472e717b74de921a4a9f3e624a3cb089","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:579 `let mut running = slot.running.lock().await;` comes before the only timeout, fleet.rs:596 `tokio::time::timeout(STOP_TIMEOUT, done)`. start() takes the same lock at fleet.rs:387 and keeps it across `self.open(inst).await` (fleet.rs:411), which ends in Core::start (fleet.rs:565). drain() (fleet.rs:673-686) stops every slot and joins them, and crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:359-361 runs sleep(drain_delay) then fleet.drain() with no outer bound. deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:23-26 budgets the stops at 10 s within terminationGracePeriodSeconds 45. Kind concern fits.","kind":"comment","references":"855134a1d21ad40050eddfcf4703cdf9501baf2fb4e1f923c1473b04d47591ff","summary":"confirmed: stop() awaits the slot lock unbounded, and start() holds that lock across open()","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.359923Z","id":"11e71cfb44922629314809f6e00a70d781555bafee2122f454dd228fb4ef7ca0","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:766-778 emits only the gauges instances_running, catalog_instances, catalog_stale and ready; the counters above it include instance_start_failures_total. `grep -n build_info crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs` finds nothing; failing instances appear only in /status (fleet.rs:482, 716), which deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml:9 says needs the peer key Prometheus is never given. Kind suggestion fits.","kind":"comment","references":"0d944b7e9e7b30a3c4b18e7ce642b5d6dad5915aeeb1584046a1decf1c181091","summary":"confirmed: /metrics exposes no failing-instances gauge and no build-info series","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T20:38:48.360748Z","id":"65e01f3b26175d90d89a33680585a051c05a36a2314263aa53064658fa0ee2c8","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:313-315 are `tokio::net::TcpListener::bind(..).await?` with no context; the error reaches main.rs:177 `eprintln!(\"clax-fleet: {e:#}\")` (or the json error at main.rs:173) as the OS message alone. Other startup checks name their setting (main.rs:251, 223, 233). Kind suggestion fits.","kind":"comment","references":"99927e7f695b60186b811a1860d94812f762e9452657a0d3c2078c5139198643","summary":"confirmed: the three binds propagate the bare io::Error","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:24.711209Z","id":"48f960efe17e4bb108297432948a244fb50885fd667316f1b76820b783d6864a","body":{"detail":"`a.ordinal.or_else(|| ordinal_of(&pod))` takes the flag or env whenever present. Env in a StatefulSet's pod template is the same for every replica, so setting CLAX_FLEET_ORDINAL there (it is listed as a plain variable in docs/kubernetes.md's table, \"this pod's shard\") gives clax-0 and clax-1 the same ordinal. Each then treats that shard's instances as its own (fleet.rs:368-373): an instance with `create = true` gets a fresh home on the second pod's volume (fleet.rs:510-517), open to /setup again, and the Service splits that tenant's requests between two divergent copies. Nothing logs that the explicit ordinal disagrees with the pod name, and no shard detects another process advertising its ordinal.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":274},"end":{"line":276},"content_hash":"9853761caa6d6c9f54513032f8482738a79994d4a271d12cc3679bedf19f6eb0"},"suggested_fix":"When both an explicit ordinal and a pod-name ordinal exist and differ, fail at startup naming both (or require an explicit `--ordinal-overrides-pod-name` style opt-in); at minimum log a warning. Document the variable as for non-StatefulSet runs only.","summary":"CLAX_FLEET_ORDINAL overrides the pod name's ordinal without any cross-check, so one value in a StatefulSet's pod template makes every pod claim the same shard and create duplicate homes","supersedes":"b5fc05c9c0ecd94ad619721a661c08b9df5124c463be64ebff4ff0b24c564438","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:01:24.875383Z","id":"4d21bf4443587af2cc07c367af443204a2619bac7424c6be3cb712367652c940","body":{"detail":"The three `TcpListener::bind(..).await?` calls propagate the io::Error as is, so the pod's last line is `clax-fleet: Address already in use (os error 98)` or `Permission denied (os error 13)` (a port under 1024 with capabilities dropped, as deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml does), without saying whether CLAX_FLEET_LISTEN, CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN or CLAX_FLEET_LOCAL_ADMIN_LISTEN is at fault. Every other startup error in serve() names its setting.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":323},"end":{"line":325},"content_hash":"51db218f53c9c7c1dbb5e093b5423a2c38ba40cf02f5e02c4d4acf7d6538d1d9"},"suggested_fix":"Wrap each bind with anyhow context naming the flag, variable and address, e.g. `.with_context(|| format!(\"--admin-listen (CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN) {admin_listen}\"))`.","summary":"A listen address that cannot be bound fails with the OS error alone, not the flag or variable that set it","supersedes":"99927e7f695b60186b811a1860d94812f762e9452657a0d3c2078c5139198643","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:14:22.063707Z","id":"c49263d2ffcbbe939eae345d51e9f67fd2a01095f8039e647d5cf55e3c98f9f1","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:04ccffad4","summary":"peer_key() (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs) now trims the key and refuses one that is not visible ASCII with an error naming CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY, and `status` uses the same check. Test tests::the_peer_key_is_trimmed_and_must_be_a_header_value, which feeds `k\\n`, ` k\\r\\n`, `a\\nb`, a BEL and non-ASCII and passes the result to Proxy::new, failed before the change (panicked at main.rs:521) and passes after it; `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/...` all PASSED.","supersedes":"b43ac9eeea5019cb94dd9ce823d29173e619651bb501dfff7501ea2de5ec4262","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:40.617085Z","id":"5394ed7bafba876458cd30fe010200f3d569200cfceeb793774d1b2e9428a967","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:7fe2de16c","summary":"shard_ordinal() in crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs now refuses an --ordinal (CLAX_FLEET_ORDINAL) that disagrees with the ordinal the pod name ends in, with an error naming both and saying to leave it unset in a StatefulSet; it still takes an explicit ordinal for a process whose name ends in none. Test tests::an_explicit_ordinal_must_agree_with_the_pod_name, written before the function existed (it did not compile), passes. The flag's help and docs/kubernetes.md's variable table (e4515f7e1) say it is for runs outside a StatefulSet. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED, and `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/...` is clean.","supersedes":"48f960efe17e4bb108297432948a244fb50885fd667316f1b76820b783d6864a","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:40.882488Z","id":"b712f1b3e7415a746d1e948e3e4405ebd0b82232f1e4429e3ae486a32a81bfb0","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:4577ee116","summary":"Fleet::drain (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs) now waits for a start under way at most STOP_TIMEOUT, through stop_after, and then logs `the instance is still starting; this process stops without it`. Fleet::stop, which the catalog's reloads use, still waits, so a paused instance never goes on running. Test fleet::tests::a_drain_waits_for_a_start_under_way_only_so_long holds a slot's lock as a start does, on tokio's paused clock. It failed before the change (panicked at fleet.rs:1034: the drain did not return within a day) and passes. docs/kubernetes.md, Draining (e4515f7e1), says so. `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED, and `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/...` is clean.","supersedes":"855134a1d21ad40050eddfcf4703cdf9501baf2fb4e1f923c1473b04d47591ff","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.365867Z","id":"8c76d8a9b0d95c950aab50b21224e7b3923bf6c76803bc0599fd467e045e9e85","body":{"detail":"CLAX_FLEET_PEER defaults to `clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480` and Peers::addr (fleet.rs:96-101) only does `replace(\"{shard}\", ..)`. A template without `{shard}` sends every shard's forwards to one host, where they come back or are refused 503 after MAX_HOPS. A StatefulSet renamed by a kustomize namePrefix (pods `prod-clax-0`), or a headless Service with another name, leaves the default pointing at names that do not resolve: every request for another shard's instance answers 502 and logs a per-request warning (proxy.rs:117-118), while /readyz stays 200 and startup says nothing. serve() already knows the pod name and its own ordinal, so it can compare `peers.addr(ordinal)`'s host with the pod name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":110},"end":{"line":116},"content_hash":"862dcbbc43e30cfe285440bc05db07c3af1e22f1669d714c4439e39ba6042159"},"suggested_fix":"At startup, fail when the template lacks `{shard}`, and warn (or fail) when the first DNS label of `peers.addr(ordinal)` is not the pod name, naming CLAX_FLEET_PEER and the expected form; optionally resolve the own peer address once and log when it does not reach this pod.","summary":"The peer template is never validated: one without `{shard}`, or whose host does not match the StatefulSet's pod names, makes every cross-shard forward fail at request time with no startup warning","supersedes":"d3dc7c58822e51a609d5b4e57142a39ce104eb535fd8eb40c4a175580fb46b83","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.508977Z","id":"9fbd50fb59e1440a954962f56a650c9f0cf01c7c6a2c68a3804de6cfdf10d148","body":{"detail":"LogFormat::Text calls `tracing_subscriber::fmt().with_env_filter(filter).init()`. tracing-subscriber 0.3.23 (Cargo.lock) is built with its default `ansi` feature (the workspace's `tracing-subscriber = { version = \"0.3\", features = [\"env-filter\"] }` in Cargo.toml does not turn defaults off), and its fmt layer emits ANSI colour codes unless NO_COLOR is set; it does not check whether stderr is a terminal. In a pod stdout is a pipe to the container runtime, so `kubectl logs` and Loki/Fluent Bit/CloudWatch store lines like `\\x1b[2m2026-...\\x1b[0m \\x1b[32m INFO\\x1b[0m`, which breaks grep and level parsing. Text is the default (main.rs:39-45) and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml sets no CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT, so this is what an operator following docs/kubernetes.md gets.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":155},"end":{"line":164},"content_hash":"1e61dc2d229fe2e7245e60a9488be0b09366b69a88a780de4e4cdc6f254afafa"},"suggested_fix":"Call `.with_ansi(std::io::IsTerminal::is_terminal(&std::io::stderr()))` (and write to stderr explicitly) on the text formatter, so colour appears only on a terminal; consider making json the default when stdout is not a terminal.","summary":"Text logs, the default, carry ANSI colour escapes into kubectl logs and cluster log collectors","supersedes":"03cf281ef1d52c49967f75567bb8244a25ab1f94ba1bec5e2f0e570234776d0b","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.644843Z","id":"e649747bf7b9df2aa810b7583ebb54d5af8066a59f3cf305f9b31129026a0b2b","body":{"detail":"`EnvFilter::try_from_default_env().unwrap_or_else(|_| EnvFilter::new(\"info\"))` discards the parse error as well as the unset case. An operator who sets RUST_LOG=clax_fleet=degub (or a directive with a typo in its target syntax) to chase a problem gets info-level logs and no word that their setting was ignored, while every other bad setting (CLAX_KEK, the peer key, the catalog) fails fast with a message naming it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":155},"end":{"line":156},"content_hash":"91ca7cd869aedef031457d347b28e5dadb78d1b5ac786b1918e4216928bc5b06"},"suggested_fix":"Distinguish the cases: when RUST_LOG is set but does not parse, either fail with `RUST_LOG: <error>` or log a warning naming the bad value right after the subscriber is installed with the fallback filter.","summary":"An unparseable RUST_LOG is silently replaced by `info`","supersedes":"de5c026dae27c8a7a849d460b9edcb12f00f944c38324c1c0753d0c32b630589","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.782475Z","id":"38207286c164d6ad7d77e4fcf99824c8b9ba7038b403b1c4be2160c709e5ea9f","body":{"detail":"The three `TcpListener::bind(..).await?` calls propagate the io::Error as is, so the pod's last line is `clax-fleet: Address already in use (os error 98)` or `Permission denied (os error 13)` (a port under 1024 with capabilities dropped, as deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml does), without saying whether CLAX_FLEET_LISTEN, CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN or CLAX_FLEET_LOCAL_ADMIN_LISTEN is at fault. Every other startup error in serve() names its setting.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":338},"end":{"line":340},"content_hash":"51db218f53c9c7c1dbb5e093b5423a2c38ba40cf02f5e02c4d4acf7d6538d1d9"},"suggested_fix":"Wrap each bind with anyhow context naming the flag, variable and address, e.g. `.with_context(|| format!(\"--admin-listen (CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN) {admin_listen}\"))`.","summary":"A listen address that cannot be bound fails with the OS error alone, not the flag or variable that set it","supersedes":"4d21bf4443587af2cc07c367af443204a2619bac7424c6be3cb712367652c940","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-02T21:53:42.923388Z","id":"f2401759914776e94dd148b04fc8be4e6c49d944b73a8f4d39c1488d7e20da21","body":{"detail":"Instances start lazily and a failed start backs off (fleet.rs:425-451); the only signal is the counter clax_fleet_instance_start_failures_total, which a rate alert sees only while requests keep arriving. /readyz answers ready whenever the process is not draining (fleet.rs:297-300), and /status, which lists `failing`, needs the peer key that Prometheus is never given (deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml's comment). Version and commit are only in /status, so `which pods run the new image` is not answerable from metrics during a RollingUpdate.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":793},"end":{"line":805},"content_hash":"43650d2692c70a574710b5992425318e9f929cf5444735126c00024f76b1c5b7"},"suggested_fix":"Add a gauge `clax_fleet_instances_failing` (slots whose `failed` is set, no names) and `clax_fleet_build_info{version=\"..\",commit=\"..\"} 1`; optionally `clax_fleet_shard{ordinal=\"..\"}` and `clax_fleet_catalog_shards` so an alert can catch ordinal >= shards (now only the warning in check_ordinal, fleet.rs:266-276).","summary":"/metrics has no gauge of instances failing to start and no build-info series, so an operator cannot alert on a shard whose instances are all down or see a rollout's progress","supersedes":"0d944b7e9e7b30a3c4b18e7ce642b5d6dad5915aeeb1584046a1decf1c181091","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:29.405006Z","id":"11d7bc52bc6a549c970bdc340032c282d1dbf8942be0ce6aba0e5871eeba9b10","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:cd3b8d32a","summary":"check_peers (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs), called from Fleet::new, refuses a peer template without {shard} when the catalog has several shards, naming CLAX_FLEET_PEER. It warns at start when this shard's host (a name, not an address or localhost) does not begin with the pod's name, naming both. Test fleet::tests::a_peer_template_is_checked_against_the_shards_and_the_pod passes; docs/kubernetes.md's variable table says so (0e6edfb48). `bazelisk test //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http:all //scripts:no_unsafe //scripts:release //scripts:version_bump` all PASSED; `bazelisk build --config=clippy //crates/clax-fleet/... //crates/clax-http/...` is clean.","supersedes":"8c76d8a9b0d95c950aab50b21224e7b3923bf6c76803bc0599fd467e045e9e85","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.451023Z","id":"4bb6d7b6ad2aadf641388424b513dac4b22112e446ee065a2898312b6508d3f0","body":{"detail":"Instances start lazily and a failed start backs off (fleet.rs:425-451); the only signal is the counter clax_fleet_instance_start_failures_total, which a rate alert sees only while requests keep arriving. /readyz answers ready whenever the process is not draining (fleet.rs:297-300), and /status, which lists `failing`, needs the peer key that Prometheus is never given (deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml's comment). Version and commit are only in /status, so `which pods run the new image` is not answerable from metrics during a RollingUpdate.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":863},"end":{"line":875},"content_hash":"43650d2692c70a574710b5992425318e9f929cf5444735126c00024f76b1c5b7"},"suggested_fix":"Add a gauge `clax_fleet_instances_failing` (slots whose `failed` is set, no names) and `clax_fleet_build_info{version=\"..\",commit=\"..\"} 1`; optionally `clax_fleet_shard{ordinal=\"..\"}` and `clax_fleet_catalog_shards` so an alert can catch ordinal >= shards (now only the warning in check_ordinal, fleet.rs:266-276).","summary":"/metrics has no gauge of instances failing to start and no build-info series, so an operator cannot alert on a shard whose instances are all down or see a rollout's progress","supersedes":"f2401759914776e94dd148b04fc8be4e6c49d944b73a8f4d39c1488d7e20da21","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.595494Z","id":"17e080aef3c8d5e6303a89a7c8a48b41197e9932d70bc9b11897e6e3b1d3cb4c","body":{"detail":"LogFormat::Text calls `tracing_subscriber::fmt().with_env_filter(filter).init()`. tracing-subscriber 0.3.23 (Cargo.lock) is built with its default `ansi` feature (the workspace's `tracing-subscriber = { version = \"0.3\", features = [\"env-filter\"] }` in Cargo.toml does not turn defaults off), and its fmt layer emits ANSI colour codes unless NO_COLOR is set; it does not check whether stderr is a terminal. In a pod stdout is a pipe to the container runtime, so `kubectl logs` and Loki/Fluent Bit/CloudWatch store lines like `\\x1b[2m2026-...\\x1b[0m \\x1b[32m INFO\\x1b[0m`, which breaks grep and level parsing. Text is the default (main.rs:39-45) and deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml sets no CLAX_FLEET_LOG_FORMAT, so this is what an operator following docs/kubernetes.md gets.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":160},"end":{"line":169},"content_hash":"1e61dc2d229fe2e7245e60a9488be0b09366b69a88a780de4e4cdc6f254afafa"},"suggested_fix":"Call `.with_ansi(std::io::IsTerminal::is_terminal(&std::io::stderr()))` (and write to stderr explicitly) on the text formatter, so colour appears only on a terminal; consider making json the default when stdout is not a terminal.","summary":"Text logs, the default, carry ANSI colour escapes into kubectl logs and cluster log collectors","supersedes":"9fbd50fb59e1440a954962f56a650c9f0cf01c7c6a2c68a3804de6cfdf10d148","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.724203Z","id":"0c9ba0be8c8cb934d9f7c751bdc2bfc486999b7fd2674aa0c3c406d383b270c8","body":{"detail":"`EnvFilter::try_from_default_env().unwrap_or_else(|_| EnvFilter::new(\"info\"))` discards the parse error as well as the unset case. An operator who sets RUST_LOG=clax_fleet=degub (or a directive with a typo in its target syntax) to chase a problem gets info-level logs and no word that their setting was ignored, while every other bad setting (CLAX_KEK, the peer key, the catalog) fails fast with a message naming it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":160},"end":{"line":161},"content_hash":"91ca7cd869aedef031457d347b28e5dadb78d1b5ac786b1918e4216928bc5b06"},"suggested_fix":"Distinguish the cases: when RUST_LOG is set but does not parse, either fail with `RUST_LOG: <error>` or log a warning naming the bad value right after the subscriber is installed with the fallback filter.","summary":"An unparseable RUST_LOG is silently replaced by `info`","supersedes":"e649747bf7b9df2aa810b7583ebb54d5af8066a59f3cf305f9b31129026a0b2b","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:11:30.851733Z","id":"89e2be562f49cd516bfd1c4b8907551da7ad164a15a5eb660a9007e67abc292c","body":{"detail":"The three `TcpListener::bind(..).await?` calls propagate the io::Error as is, so the pod's last line is `clax-fleet: Address already in use (os error 98)` or `Permission denied (os error 13)` (a port under 1024 with capabilities dropped, as deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml does), without saying whether CLAX_FLEET_LISTEN, CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN or CLAX_FLEET_LOCAL_ADMIN_LISTEN is at fault. Every other startup error in serve() names its setting.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":344},"end":{"line":346},"content_hash":"51db218f53c9c7c1dbb5e093b5423a2c38ba40cf02f5e02c4d4acf7d6538d1d9"},"suggested_fix":"Wrap each bind with anyhow context naming the flag, variable and address, e.g. `.with_context(|| format!(\"--admin-listen (CLAX_FLEET_ADMIN_LISTEN) {admin_listen}\"))`.","summary":"A listen address that cannot be bound fails with the OS error alone, not the flag or variable that set it","supersedes":"38207286c164d6ad7d77e4fcf99824c8b9ba7038b403b1c4be2160c709e5ea9f","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:36:24.022790Z","id":"4c9aebf04c25878e02bb68878b04d335b316457f29e02de57d6b401978a97899","body":{"detail":"deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml gives the container the clax-secrets Secret twice: as `envFrom: secretRef` (read once, at pod start) and mounted at /etc/clax-secrets (CLAX_FLEET_SECRETS_DIR). read_secret takes the file when it exists and otherwise falls back to `env(var)`. Rotation works (the new file wins), but removal does not: after `kubectl patch secret clax-secrets` drops CLAX_SETUP_SECRET_ACME (to close /setup) or CLAX_GITHUB_SECRET_ACME (a leaked OAuth secret), the kubelet removes the file, and the next start of the instance (pause and unpause, docs/kubernetes.md 'Instances' secrets') reads the old value from the environment the pod started with. The same silent fallback hides a misconfigured mount: `std::fs::read_to_string(..).ok()` turns EACCES, a non-UTF-8 file or a CLAX_FLEET_SECRETS_DIR that does not exist into 'no file' with nothing logged, and the stale environment value is used. docs/kubernetes.md says only 'from the environment when there is no such file', never that a removed key lingers.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":142},"end":{"line":155},"content_hash":"90474d3cef279815cbecead43068602941130e5c0225ab74173fd9479301b225"},"suggested_fix":"When a secrets directory is given and exists, treat it as authoritative for instance secrets: a missing file means no secret (do not fall back to the environment), and fall back only when no directory is configured. Log (once per var) any read error other than NotFound, and refuse to start (or warn loudly at startup) when CLAX_FLEET_SECRETS_DIR names a directory that does not exist. Add a test in fleet.rs's tests: a var present in env, absent from the directory, reads as None.","summary":"A secret removed from clax-secrets is still read from the pod's environment, so deleting a tenant's key does not revoke it until the pod restarts","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:36:24.022871Z","id":"4d8ab20e1c0f0adcec38b1827fa440017584766db582f977b6c34b58184b0bcc","body":{"detail":"dispatch believes HOP_HEADER only beside a matching KEY_HEADER (`let hops = if peer { hops(&req) } else { 0 }`), and MAX_HOPS (fleet.rs:61) is the only loop guard. Rotating CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY takes `kubectl rollout restart sts clax` (docs/kubernetes.md, 'Instances' secrets'), one pod at a time, so for the whole rollout pods hold different keys; a ConfigMap edit reaches each pod up to a minute apart, so catalogs disagree meanwhile. With pod clax-0 (key K1, catalog: instance X on shard 1) and clax-1 (key K2, catalog: X on shard 0), a client request for X at clax-0 is forwarded to clax-1 with hop 1; clax-1 does not believe it (hops 0), resolves X by Host to shard 0 and forwards to clax-0 with hop 1; clax-0 does not believe that either, and so on. Each hop holds a connection on both pods for up to proxy::RESPONSE_TIMEOUT (MAX_WAIT_SECS + 60 = 3660 s), so one request grows an unbounded chain of connections. A key mismatch alone is otherwise silent: forwards still work by Host, so nothing reports that peers no longer trust each other.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1002},"end":{"line":1008},"content_hash":"89794a0e112c4cd22088277f85564ff8620c306e331e8a25bbf7c756a1ed8f98"},"suggested_fix":"Separate loop prevention from trust: honor HOP_HEADER from any sender as a floor on the hop count (a client that sets it can only stop its own request from being forwarded), and keep INSTANCE_HEADER key-gated; that is, compute `hops = parsed hop header` always, and take the INSTANCE_HEADER path only when `peer && hops > 0`. Count requests that carry KEY_HEADER with a wrong key in a metric (and log once), so a key mismatch shows up in /metrics. Add a shards.rs test with two processes given different peer keys and disagreeing catalogs that asserts a 503, not a hang.","summary":"A forward whose peer key the receiving pod does not share has its hop count reset to 0, so two pods whose keys and catalogs disagree forward one request back and forth without bound","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:36:24.022934Z","id":"dd104c6268350e90b2daa5d8fd66d3d75b7e19b3861e3275f1c70f3dab0320ff","body":{"detail":"check_peers refuses only a multi-shard template without `{shard}`. CLAX_FLEET_PEER=`clax-{shard}.clax-peers` (no port) passes silently: `rsplit_once(':')` is None, the whole string is the host, and its first label equals the pod's name; proxy::forward then builds `http://clax-1.clax-peers/...`, which connects to port 80 where the pod listens on 7480 (deploy/k8s/sqlite/peers.yaml), so every cross-shard request is 502. CLAX_FLEET_PEER=`http://clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480` yields only the namePrefix warning (its first label is `http://clax-0`), whose text blames the pod name, and every forward then fails building `http://http://...` (proxy.rs:232). Either mistake surfaces only as forward_errors_total climbing after deploy.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":111},"end":{"line":140},"content_hash":"785310829770b57bc6301f3accdf4988c4c30aa3cb3876ab765128964d27101c"},"suggested_fix":"In check_peers, render the template for this ordinal and require it to parse as an `axum::http::uri::Authority` with an explicit port (`authority.port_u16().is_some()`), refusing startup otherwise with a message naming CLAX_FLEET_PEER and the `host:port` form; extend a_peer_template_is_checked_against_the_shards_and_the_pod with the no-port and with-scheme cases.","summary":"The peer template check passes a template without a port and only warns on one with a scheme, both of which make every forward fail","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:36:24.022976Z","id":"29a206f7e678b7244e9318f840307e0160ab5407a455a584e2ee1682cd76feee","body":{"detail":"open() makes a home wherever the catalog entry's shard says whenever `inst.create` is set, with no notion of whether the instance already has data on another shard. docs/kubernetes.md ('Moving an instance') promises 'The new shard never makes an empty instance in its place, because without create = true a missing home keeps the instance down', yet the shipped example catalog (deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml:19) and the scaling-out instructions set `create = true` and no step says to remove it. So with create left on, a mistyped `shard` in step 3, a copy into the wrong path, or the 'shard set back by mistake' that step 4 guards against each make a fresh home on the wrong pod: the tenant's hosts are answered by an empty instance whose /setup opens with the still-valid setup secret, and new writes land on a shard the tenant's real data is not on.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":574},"end":{"line":584},"content_hash":"1f0863bf18d52d1d02422d56494d328b6ef4eba8b21c94109a997fe56ad0223c"},"suggested_fix":"Make create one-shot in code: on a catalog reload, refuse (keep the previous catalog in effect and report it in /status and clax_fleet_catalog_stale) any entry whose shard changed while `create = true`; and in open(), when creating a home, log at warn that a new home was made so the event is visible. In docs/kubernetes.md, add 'remove create = true' after an instance's first start and to step 3 of Moving an instance, and drop it from the example's comment once the instance exists.","summary":"`create = true` stays in effect after an instance's first start, so a shard change applied before (or without) moving its home starts an empty instance at the tenant's origin","tags":["review","review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:56.536371Z","id":"c34f6b297794398ffd496c2678a54cebee9d90583c25e808cfe6c9fd944a91b8","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:149-154: read_secret maps any read_to_string error to None via `.ok()` and returns `in_dir.or_else(|| env(var))`; fleet.rs:599-600 passes `std::env::var(var).ok()` as env. deploy/k8s/sqlite/statefulset.yaml:81-83 loads the same Secret by `envFrom: secretRef: clax-secrets` and :117-119/:129-131 mounts it at /etc/clax-secrets (CLAX_FLEET_SECRETS_DIR, :64-65). Environment variables are fixed at container start, so once the kubelet drops the removed key's file, the next open() of the instance takes the startup value. docs/kubernetes.md:211-214 says only 'from the environment when there is no such file'. Kind concern is right: an operational revocation gap, not a pre-merge defect in the path the docs describe.","kind":"comment","references":"4c9aebf04c25878e02bb68878b04d335b316457f29e02de57d6b401978a97899","summary":"confirmed: a key removed from clax-secrets is still read from the pod's environment","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:56.536835Z","id":"e7dea2921d70892606df8248e4831e25ee2c3dd6d0d1698a7e1d663092182f19","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1006-1007: `let peer = fleet.is_peer(req.headers()); let hops = if peer { hops(&req) } else { 0 };` then :1022-1028 resolves by Host. fleet.rs:432 `can_forward = hops < MAX_HOPS` (MAX_HOPS = 2, :61) is the only stop, and fleet.rs:406 forwards with `hops + 1`, so a receiver with a different CLAX_FLEET_PEER_KEY sees hop 0 every time. proxy.rs:66-67 sets RESPONSE_TIMEOUT to MAX_WAIT_SECS + 60, and proxy.rs:234-237 waits that long for headers on each hop. The loop needs keys and catalogs to disagree at once (a key rotation by rolling restart overlapping a catalog change), so concern is the right kind.","kind":"comment","references":"4d8ab20e1c0f0adcec38b1827fa440017584766db582f977b6c34b58184b0bcc","summary":"confirmed: a forward with an unrecognized peer key restarts at hop 0, so MAX_HOPS does not bound it","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:56.537265Z","id":"c396243e655c88b61c711acc37bba619879e1c80da7d898a5931bc75aefbd95d","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:128: `addr.rsplit_once(':').map_or(addr.as_str(), ...)` takes the whole of `clax-1.clax-peers` as the host; its first label equals the pod (:133-135), so Ok(None). proxy.rs:232 then builds `http://{addr}{pq}`, which defaults to port 80, while deploy/k8s/sqlite/peers.yaml:15-17 exposes 7480. With `http://clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480` the first label is `http://clax-0`, so :137-139 warns that the address 'is not this pod', and proxy.rs:232 forms `http://http://clax-1...`, which cannot reach the peer. No other check of CLAX_FLEET_PEER exists (crates/clax-fleet/src/main.rs:111-116 only reads it; the default `clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480` is correct). Concern is right: only an overridden template is affected.","kind":"comment","references":"dd104c6268350e90b2daa5d8fd66d3d75b7e19b3861e3275f1c70f3dab0320ff","summary":"confirmed: check_peers accepts a template without a port and only warns on one with a scheme","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T03:37:56.537707Z","id":"1d276424e8e1de86792cd05be582cfdfd23c84c3a6b5def319e0b1c3360b53f2","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:578 refuses a missing home only when `!inst.create`; otherwise :585-591 make one. crates/clax-fleet/src/catalog.rs does nothing with `create` beyond reading and comparing it (lines 68, 85, 126, 345). deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml:19 and docs/kubernetes.md:141 ship `create = true`, :275 says to give new instances `create = true`, and the Moving an instance steps (docs/kubernetes.md:291-311) never say to remove it, while :291-292 promises the new shard never makes an empty instance. Concern is right: it needs an operator mistake or an unchanged create flag during a move.","kind":"comment","references":"29a206f7e678b7244e9318f840307e0160ab5407a455a584e2ee1682cd76feee","summary":"confirmed: create = true keeps making a fresh home on whichever shard the catalog names","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.679145Z","id":"c5779ef7065b402e6618cabdad16d2592f769efa75709410605ec0236bf4c306","body":{"detail":"Commit 0e370fbb3 'fix(fleet): a secrets directory is the only source of instance secrets' (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs read_secret and check_secrets_dir, called from Fleet::new; docs/kubernetes.md, Instances' secrets, says how to revoke). Test: fleet.rs tests::a_secrets_directory_is_the_only_source_of_instance_secrets asserts a variable present in the environment but absent from the directory reads as None, an empty or unreadable file as None, and check_secrets_dir refusing a missing directory with a message naming CLAX_FLEET_SECRETS_DIR. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/...` at 0e370fbb3: clax_fleet_lib_test PASSED, clax-fleet_bin_test PASSED, integration PASSED. Without a directory the environment is still read (asserted in the same test).","kind":"comment","references":"4c9aebf04c25878e02bb68878b04d335b316457f29e02de57d6b401978a97899","summary":"fixed: with CLAX_FLEET_SECRETS_DIR set, read_secret reads only the directory; a missing file is no secret, an unreadable one is logged, and a secrets directory that is not one refuses startup","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.679779Z","id":"a5b3a9709b6f30893f807c8fe1560e4131dca54eb0eeaa1d7064c2227ab95975","body":{"detail":"Commit e79e8800f 'fix(fleet): refuse a peer template that is not host:port': check_peers parses this shard's rendered address as axum::http::uri::Authority and requires port_u16(), refusing with a message naming CLAX_FLEET_PEER and the host:port form. Test: fleet.rs tests::a_peer_template_is_checked_against_the_shards_and_the_pod now asserts Err for clax-{shard}.clax-peers, http://clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480, clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480/, clax-{shard}.clax-peers:port and user@clax-{shard}.clax-peers:7480, and still Ok for the default and loopback/IPv6 templates. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/...` at e79e8800f: all three targets PASSED. docs/kubernetes.md's variable table says host:port, no scheme.","kind":"comment","references":"dd104c6268350e90b2daa5d8fd66d3d75b7e19b3861e3275f1c70f3dab0320ff","summary":"fixed: check_peers refuses a CLAX_FLEET_PEER that does not render to host:port (no port, a scheme, a path, userinfo) at startup","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.680765Z","id":"1af903c28261ae3194eb53652f687a66e3a97f9365db4f7e829f4746662476ac","body":{"detail":"Commit 4fc0d860e 'fix(fleet): refuse a catalog that moves an instance while create = true' (fleet.rs moved_with_create, used by Fleet::reload; open() warns 'making a new, empty home'; deploy/k8s/base/fleet.toml and docs/kubernetes.md, The catalog and Moving an instance, say to remove create = true once started and before a move). Test: crates/clax-fleet/tests/shards.rs a_shard_change_with_create_set_is_refused: with acme running on shard 0, a catalog moving it to shard 1 with create = true leaves both pods' /status catalog.error naming acme and create = true, clax_fleet_catalog_stale 1, acme still served from shard 0 and no home made on shard 1; the same move without create takes effect and shard 1 answers 503 without making a home. Against the base fleet.rs this test fails (catalog.error null); on 4fc0d860e it passes (`bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/...`, integration PASSED). The remainder, a pod that starts (or restarts) with a catalog that already moved the instance under create = true, is recorded as a new concern on fleet.rs moved_with_create.","kind":"comment","references":"29a206f7e678b7244e9318f840307e0160ab5407a455a584e2ee1682cd76feee","summary":"fixed in part: a reload that changes an instance's shard while create = true is refused (previous catalog stays, /status and clax_fleet_catalog_stale say why), a new home is logged at warn, and the docs say to remove create after the first start; a pod that starts with such a catalog is not covered","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.681647Z","id":"5dc1859fca22dfbe6d320b37eafee0d277a0924a158466b8e9ea7caaada17243","body":{"detail":"Commit 432cbef8a 'fix(fleet): count a forward's hops whatever its peer key' (fleet.rs dispatch and Fleet::check_peer_key; proxy.rs module docs; docs/kubernetes.md Security and Operating it). Tests in crates/clax-fleet/tests/shards.rs: processes_whose_keys_and_catalogs_disagree_do_not_pass_a_request_on_forever (pods with keys key-0/key-1 and catalogs placing acme on each other's shard: the request ends 503 after one forward each, and each counts one key mismatch) and a_clients_hop_count_only_stops_its_own_forward (a client's hop 2 keeps globex from being forwarded, 503 not 502, and its instance header is ignored: acme.test is served by Host). Against the base fleet.rs the first ended 502 after 3.5 s of forwarding (left: 502, right: 503) and the second answered 502; with the commit both pass (`bazel test //crates/clax-fleet/...`: integration PASSED).","kind":"comment","references":"4d8ab20e1c0f0adcec38b1827fa440017584766db582f977b6c34b58184b0bcc","summary":"fixed: the hop count is believed from anyone as a floor, the instance header stays key-gated, and a wrong peer key is counted in clax_fleet_peer_key_mismatches_total and logged once","tags":["session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:26.686669Z","id":"874b39cc3546f89ce601602022dd3edd8808d9a8bfdc18c1259bae229a107d9f","body":{"detail":"moved_with_create (crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:1020-1035) refuses, in Fleet::reload, a catalog that gives an instance of the catalog in effect another shard while create = true. Fleet::new loads the mounted catalog with nothing to compare it to, so a pod that starts or restarts after such a ConfigMap change (a rollout, an evicted pod, a pod that was down when the edit landed) takes the catalog and, finding no home on its shard, makes an empty one (fleet.rs:643, the create branch of open()), answering the tenant's hosts with /setup open to the setup secret. docs/kubernetes.md (Moving an instance) tells the operator to remove create = true before a move, which is the only guard in that case. Failure: catalog edits instance acme from shard 0 to 1 with create = true kept; pod clax-1 restarts before or after the edit reaches it; clax-1 makes /var/lib/clax/instances/acme empty.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1020},"end":{"line":1035},"content_hash":"6a257ffd9b7d4d9238dbbf642f776d684f78932b9149b9068116acd3d6d5d909"},"suggested_fix":"Make create one-shot on disk rather than in the catalog comparison: once a home is made, record it beside the catalog the pods share (for example a per-instance marker each pod can see through its peers' /status, or a field the fleet writes back to a ConfigMap), or require create to carry the shard it was given for (create = <shard>) so a changed shard with a stale create is refused at load time on every pod, Fleet::new included; check-catalog would then refuse it too.","summary":"A pod that starts with a catalog that already moved an instance under create = true still makes an empty home: moved_with_create only compares against the catalog a running pod has in effect","tags":["review:k8s-day-two-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/proxy.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:27.458295Z","id":"a209d6e458ef7443f40e649a46213aaba7937f2a787c67429665a6b6d2d7d34e","body":{"detail":"Hosted mode counts its abuse limits by client address: CF-Connecting-IP behind Cloudflare ([hosted] behind_cloudflare), else the connection's peer, which for a forwarded request is another pod and behind an Ingress is the controller for every client. Off Cloudflare, every client shares one limit. A trusted-proxy setting that reads X-Forwarded-For from known peers would fix it.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged after the lines moved; originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0).","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":94},"end":{"line":102},"content_hash":"ca1d4399bd72b21af290e83f8069a0b6c39cb86c4b7dc057bf7de56a39d0aee5"},"summary":"A forwarded request reaches its owner with the forwarding pod as its peer","supersedes":"47521bca0d0a37b3b358dbc30cbed8cd37963bb9f7729ae2bdb02fac6bedd02e","tags":["security","ops","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-11-03T04:36:27.757839Z","id":"84e5d44f9bc744299c004e563b1b794ed34fc1c6550f111e5f60fef1cb52eec2","body":{"detail":"Instances start lazily and a failed start backs off (fleet.rs:425-451); the only signal is the counter clax_fleet_instance_start_failures_total, which a rate alert sees only while requests keep arriving. /readyz answers ready whenever the process is not draining (fleet.rs:297-300), and /status, which lists `failing`, needs the peer key that Prometheus is never given (deploy/k8s/base/networkpolicy.yaml's comment). Version and commit are only in /status, so `which pods run the new image` is not answerable from metrics during a RollingUpdate.\n\nRe-anchored unchanged after the lines moved; originally recorded by mailto:alex@kesling.co (session:claude-code:cee68005-68c6-43ca-8000-8b393af6e6ea).","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":942},"end":{"line":959},"content_hash":"d1d6f89fe8f78722cfa82861d34c7d36e432b4e9372d02ed97cb6e5ba9b76078"},"suggested_fix":"Add a gauge `clax_fleet_instances_failing` (slots whose `failed` is set, no names) and `clax_fleet_build_info{version=\"..\",commit=\"..\"} 1`; optionally `clax_fleet_shard{ordinal=\"..\"}` and `clax_fleet_catalog_shards` so an alert can catch ordinal >= shards (now only the warning in check_ordinal, fleet.rs:266-276).","summary":"/metrics has no gauge of instances failing to start and no build-info series, so an operator cannot alert on a shard whose instances are all down or see a rollout's progress","supersedes":"4bb6d7b6ad2aadf641388424b513dac4b22112e446ee065a2898312b6508d3f0","tags":["review","review:k8s-ergonomics-2026-11-02","session:claude-code:6339925b-eee1-47c9-bafb-ab7115d6c4da"]}}
